/* ── MODERN FOCUSED, ROOMS PAGE: A STILL HEADER AND NO META ROW (2026-09-24) ────────────
   Requested: "modern focused view - Rooms page - remove the movement from the header and hide
   the subheader". Basic already reads this way for its whole org section
   (`body.ll-view-basic .md-hero--org`, _classic-home.scss); this is the same treatment, asked
   for on one page of one layout, so it is scoped to that page rather than folded in there.

   THE MOTION RULES CANNOT SIMPLY BE SWITCHED OFF -- the same trap the Basic rule records.
   `.md-hero__title--from-left` (app.scss:34501) sets `opacity: 0` and animates to visible with
   `forwards`, so the animation is what REVEALS the title, not just what moves it. `animation:
   none` alone would leave the header permanently invisible, which is why `opacity: 1` comes
   with it. The divider is static and is deliberately left alone (app.scss:34591).

   ONLY THE TITLE NEEDS THE MOTION RULE. The subheader's own `--from-right` span is inside the
   `--meta` row being hidden below, so a second animation rule for it would be dead.

   `:has(...)` NAMES THE ROOMS PAGE BY WHAT IT RENDERS, because the hero lives in the PARENT
   template (templates/organization.hbs) and the page itself has no class to hang this on. The
   three arguments are that page's three content states: `.md-stats-main` (the supervisor's room
   list), `.md-org-rooms` and `.md-org-rooms-empty` (the manager's list and its empty state) --
   verified unique to templates/organization/rooms.hbs. The loading state is deliberately not
   listed; it is transient and matching it would change nothing a user can see.
   `.md-hero--org` in the descendant part keeps this off the Reports page, which also carries
   `.md-stats-main` but has no org hero.

   BOTH VIEWS SINCE 2026-10-01 (was `body.ll-view-modern.ll-layout-focused`): Basic's rooms pages
   now show their header as Modern's do (requested; see `md-hero--org-rooms` in _classic-home.scss),
   so in Focused they take this same still, meta-less list header.

   NO `!important`: at (0,6,1) against the base rules' (0,3,0) this wins on specificity alone,
   which matters because this partial is `@use`d and so EMITTED BEFORE app.scss -- a tie would
   have gone to the base rule. */
body.ll-layout-focused .md-workspace:has(.md-stats-main, .md-org-rooms, .md-org-rooms-empty) .md-hero--org .md-hero__title .md-hero__title--from-left {
  animation: none;
  opacity: 1;
}

body.ll-layout-focused .md-workspace:has(.md-stats-main, .md-org-rooms, .md-org-rooms-empty) .md-hero--org .md-hero__sub--meta {
  display: none;
}

