body.ll-layout-focused .nb-section {
  /* SILVER, NOT BLUE. The first attempt reused `$focus-section-bg-darkened`, and it read
     blue for the reason this file already documents at ~line 170: darkening a COOL
     near-white raises its apparent saturation, because the R-B spread widens as the stop
     darkens. That note records 20% giving rgb(167, 188, 217) and 15% giving
     rgb(184, 201, 224) -- "both read BLUE". The same trap caught this card: its top stop
     resolved to rgb(206, 221, 236), a 30-point R-B spread.
     So the ramp is authored directly at the lightness wanted instead of being derived by
     darkening a tinted base, which puts the R-B spread exactly where it is wanted rather
     than wherever the darkening maths lands it.
     TUNED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. The first silver pass landed at 7 / 6 / 3 and read GREY --
     overcorrected, having come straight from 30 / 27 / 0, which read blue. 16 / 12 / 8 is
     the middle: enough cool cast to be silver rather than a neutral grey, still less than
     half the spread that made it look like a blue tile. Values computed, not eyeballed,
     as the note at ~170 asks. */
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgb(216, 221, 232) 0%, rgb(226, 230, 238) 45%, rgb(235, 238, 243) 100%);
  /* Neutral ink, not `$focus-accent`. The interior falloff used dusty denim, which was
     painting blue INSIDE the card on top of the blue gradient -- the two together are
     what made it read as a blue tile. A plain grey does the same shading work without
     carrying a hue. */
  border: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.1);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85), inset 0 10px 20px rgba(120, 125, 132, 0.1), inset 0 -1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.5), 0 2px 4px rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.08), 0 10px 24px rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.12), 0 20px 48px rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.1);
}

body.ll-layout-focused .nb-section__title {
