/* ORGANISATION SWITCHER above the org header (2026-09-23), on a card that matches the room
   buttons below it (requested): label on the left, dropdown on the right.

   THE CARD VALUES ARE THE ROOM CARD'S, restated rather than borrowed by class. `.md-room-card`
   is scoped to `.md-grid .md-card--rooms` and `.md-shell--org .md-rooms__grid` -- this control
   sits ABOVE the header, outside both, so wearing that class would have styled nothing. They
   are the same gradient, border, radius, padding and lift, so the two read as one family.

   The select does NOT wear `.ch-tabselect`: that class is `display: none` until 550px, where
   it stands in for the tab strip, so reusing it would hide this control at every normal width.
   Its own look follows the app's modern-select idiom (`appearance: none` plus an inline-SVG
   chevron), the same one `.md-preferences__form select.form-control` uses -- Bootstrap's native
   select chrome is suppressed rather than inherited. */
.ch-org-switcher {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: 16px;
  /* PUSHED DOWN, AND CLOSER TO THE TITLE (requested 2026-09-23). The 28px above separates it
     from the tab strip; the 0 below hands the remaining gap entirely to the hero's own
     padding, which is tightened just beneath.
     Reads --dn-org-switcher-top, which the Focused rooms list sets to 0 in both views (2026-10-01;
     _focused-view.scss): its "Rooms - <org>" page label sits right above and carries the gap. */
  margin: var(--dn-org-switcher-top, 28px) auto 0;
  width: fit-content;
  max-width: 100%;
  padding: 14px 16px;
  border-radius: 14px;
  background: radial-gradient(125% 125% at 26% 18%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95), transparent 60%), linear-gradient(140deg, #ffffff 0%, #eef0f4 52%, #e2e6ec 100%);
  border: 1px solid rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.2);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95), inset 0 -3px 5px rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.1), 0 3px 9px rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.08), 0 0 16px rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.16);
}

/* CLEAR OF THE FLOATING NAV, WHERE THERE IS ONE (2026-09-23, with the switcher's move into
   Modern). Gentle's app shell gives `.md-shell` no top padding and pulls the pill nav out of
   flow by its own height, so the page box starts at y=106 while the nav's underside is at 146 --
   the nav deliberately floats over the first 40px of the page. Every other page's first element
   is text that begins lower down, so nothing showed; this one is a CARD with a background, and
   at the base 28px it sat visibly behind the pills (measured: switcher 134-204, nav 70-146).
   68px = the 40px the nav overlaps + the same 28px the control already has above it in Basic,
   so it keeps its spacing rather than being given a new one. Measured after: switcher at 174,
   28px clear of the nav.
   `:has(.ll-appshell__navbar)` is the same test the shell's own alignment rules use for "is
   there a nav floating over this page" (app.scss), so the two cannot disagree. Basic renders no
   app shell at all, so it never matches and keeps the 28px above.
   BOTH LAYOUTS, and the reason is worth stating because it briefly was not: this clearance is
   owed wherever the shell's own nav clearance is ZERO and the nav therefore floats over the
   page. That was gentle only until the org pages were pointed at gentle's geometry in
   _focused-view.scss, and it is now true of them in focused as well -- so the condition to test
   is the floating nav, which `:has(.ll-appshell__navbar)` already names, and not the layout. */
#content:has(.ll-appshell__navbar) .ch-org-switcher {
  /* The Modern Focused Rooms list sets this to 0 (--dn-org-switcher-top,
     _focused-view.scss): its page label sits above the switcher and already clears the nav. */
  margin-top: var(--dn-org-switcher-top, 68px);
}

.ch-org-switcher__label {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: #1B365D;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

/* THE DROPDOWN. A <details> rather than a <select>, because the requirement was an on-brand
   OPEN LIST and a native select's popup belongs to the operating system.
   Its own classes: `.ch-tabselect` and `.md-pillnav-dropdown` are each `display: none` until
   550px, where they stand in for a tab strip, so reusing either would have hidden this at every
   normal width -- the same trap twice over. The trigger keeps the modern-select LOOK
   (white field, hairline border, inline chevron) so it still reads as a select. */
.ch-org-dropdown {
  position: relative;
  flex: 0 1 auto;
  min-width: 200px;
  max-width: 100%;
}

.ch-org-dropdown__trigger {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: 10px;
  height: 40px;
  padding: 0 12px;
  /* Radius, border and hover taken from `.md-org-report-select` (app.scss ~97479), the
     reports page's control, so the two org pages offer the same-looking select. Those rules
     are view-agnostic -- the reports page has no focused/gentle split, every one of its 52
     rules living in app.scss -- so matching them covers both layouts by construction rather
     than needing a second set here. */
  border-radius: 12px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.12);
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 2px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.05);
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: #1B365D;
  cursor: pointer;
  list-style: none;
}
.ch-org-dropdown__trigger::-webkit-details-marker {
  display: none;
}
.ch-org-dropdown__trigger::marker {
  content: "";
}

.ch-org-dropdown__trigger:hover {
  border-color: rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.3);
  box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.1);
}

.ch-org-dropdown__current {
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

.ch-org-dropdown__chevron {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  transition: transform 0.15s ease;
}

.ch-org-dropdown[open] .ch-org-dropdown__chevron {
  transform: rotate(180deg);
}

/* The panel. Absolutely positioned so opening it does not push the page title down, and above
   the hero beneath it. */
.ch-org-dropdown__list {
  position: absolute;
  z-index: 20;
  top: calc(100% + 6px);
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 6px;
  list-style: none;
  border-radius: 12px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.2);
  background: #fff;
  box-shadow: 0 8px 28px rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.16), 0 2px 6px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.06);
  max-height: 320px;
  overflow-y: auto;
}

.ch-org-dropdown__option {
  display: block;
  padding: 10px 12px;
  border-radius: 8px;
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: #1B365D;
  text-decoration: none;
  min-height: 40px;
  line-height: 20px;
}

.ch-org-dropdown__option:hover,
.ch-org-dropdown__option:focus-visible {
  background: rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.1);
  text-decoration: none;
}

/* The org already open. Marked with a background and full-strength ink rather than dimmed --
   the current row is the one a person is most likely scanning for. */
.ch-org-dropdown__option.is-current {
  background: rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.06);
  cursor: default;
}

/* THE GAP BELOW THE SWITCHER IS MOSTLY THE HERO'S, not the switcher's. `.md-hero--org` carries
   `padding: 20px 2px 10px !important` (app.scss), so trimming the switcher's own margin alone
   would still have left 20px of hero padding between the two. This takes that top padding down
   while leaving the sides and bottom exactly as they are -- a shorthand here would have reset
   them for a change that only concerns the top.

   `:has(.ch-org-switcher)` SCOPES IT TO THE CASE THAT ASKED FOR IT. The switcher only renders
   when there is more than one organisation; without this the title would pull up on every org
   page, including the ones with no switcher above it to close the gap to.
   BOTH VIEWS AS OF 2026-09-23, when the switcher itself stopped being Basic-only (see
   `showOrgSwitcher` in controllers/organization.js) -- but NOT at the same value, so each view
   states its own rather than one inheriting the other's and being corrected afterwards.
   BASIC KEEPS 6px. Its switcher sits under the in-flow `ch-tabs` strip, and this pairing was
   tuned against that (with its own 22px at 1201px and up, further down this file).
   MODERN TAKES 22px (requested: "there needs to be a bit more space below the dropdown on the
   rooms page"). Its switcher hangs below a nav that FLOATS over the page, so it reads as a
   card on open space rather than as the second row of a strip, and the tight pairing that
   suits Basic looks cramped here. 22px is Basic's own large-screen value for this same pair,
   so the two views agree on a number rather than inventing a second one. */
body.ll-view-basic .md-workspace:has(.ch-org-switcher) .md-hero--org {
  padding-top: 6px !important;
}

body.ll-view-modern .md-workspace:has(.ch-org-switcher) .md-hero--org {
  padding-top: 22px !important;
}

/* ROOM SUMMARY CARDS (2026-09-23). The org room page reuses the Reports page's `.report-kpi`,
   but its figures are not headline numbers -- they are phrases like "0 words, 0 sessions" and
   "0 words/day, 0 sessions/wk". At the Reports page's 25-32px they wrapped to three lines and
   read as a headline the value cannot carry.

   SO THE PAIR IS INVERTED HERE (requested): the LABEL leads, in the steadier weight, and the
   figure sits under it at a size a phrase can actually hold. The markup order is swapped to
   match, so this is not CSS re-ordering what the DOM says -- a screen reader hears the label
   first too.

   `.room-kpi` is an extra class on the same element, so the card keeps every other rule it
   inherits, including the focused surface. Only type and order change. */
/* EQUAL HEIGHT ACROSS THE FOUR. They sit in Bootstrap `col-sm-3` columns, which do not
   stretch their children, so each card was only as tall as its own text and the row came out
   ragged. A min-height equalises them without making the row a flex container -- that row also
   holds the panels and charts below, and turning it flex would have re-laid those out too. */
.room-kpi {
  min-height: 92px;
}

.room-kpi .report-kpi__label {
  margin: 0;
  color: var(--report-ink);
  /* 14px IS A FLOOR, not a preference. It was briefly taken to 13px while shrinking the pair,
     which is below the size this product holds itself to -- its users include people reading
     at distance, on mounted devices, and with low vision, and the label is the part that says
     what the figure means. Shrink the VALUE if the row needs more room; this does not go
     under 14. */
  font-size: 14px;
  font-weight: 700;
  line-height: 1.3;
}

.room-kpi .report-kpi__value {
  /* HAIRLINE BETWEEN LABEL AND FIGURE. Carried as a `border-top` on the VALUE rather than a
     `border-bottom` on the label, so it spans the value's box: the label often wraps to two
     short lines and a rule under it would end mid-card, which is what reads as unfinished
     rather than deliberate.
     Low-alpha navy at 0.12 -- the same weight the reports page's own divider uses -- so it
     separates without competing with either line it sits between. */
  margin: 7px 0 0;
  padding-top: 7px;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.12);
  color: var(--report-muted);
  /* 15px, against the Reports page's clamp(25px, 2.6vw, 32px). These are not headline numbers
     but phrases -- "0 words/day, 0 sessions/wk" -- and at the headline size they wrapped to
     three lines in a quarter-width column, which is what made the row ragged in the first
     place. Smaller is what lets them sit on one or two lines. */
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 650;
  letter-spacing: normal;
  line-height: 1.35;
}

/* LIST-SHAPED CARDS (Goal Words, Curriculum & Training). These two hold a word list or a
   curriculum name plus an action link rather than a single figure, so their value slot is a
   <div> of flowing content instead of a one-line <p>.

   Kept SCROLLABLE rather than clipped: the original panel body carried
   `max-height: 90px; overflow: auto`, and a room with a dozen goal words still needs to reach
   all of them. Dropping that in the name of a tidy card would have hidden data.
   `min-width: 0` is what lets the words wrap inside the grid's `minmax(0, 1fr)` track instead
   of forcing the card wider than its column. */
.room-kpi--list .report-kpi__value {
  max-height: 84px;
  overflow-y: auto;
  min-width: 0;
  /* The inline styles the original markup puts on its word spans (16px bold) still apply and
     are left alone -- they are the words' own emphasis, not the card's. */
}

.room-kpi--list .report-kpi__value a {
  font-size: 14px;
}

/* TWO PER ROW AT 1200px AND BELOW (requested 2026-09-23). The cards sit in Bootstrap
   `col-sm-3 col-xs-6` columns -- four across from 768px up -- and at a quarter of the width
   their phrase-shaped values wrap hard. Half-width gives them a line to sit on.

   THE COLUMN IS WIDENED, NOT THE CARD. `width` lives on the `col-sm-3`; setting it on
   `.room-kpi` would leave the card overflowing a 25% column rather than the column resizing.
   `:has(> .room-kpi)` is what reaches it from the only class this page owns -- and it keeps the
   rule off every other `col-sm-3` in the app, including the ones further down this same page.

   (0,2,0) against Bootstrap's `.col-sm-3` at (0,1,0), so it wins on specificity and not on
   source order, which matters because Bootstrap's own rule lives in a `min-width: 768px` query
   that overlaps this one across 768-1200. Below 768 `col-xs-6` already gives two per row, so
   the two agree rather than fight. */
/* The break is a 1200px-and-below device only: above it the columns are a quarter each and the
   action column plus both list cards still fit one line, which is the layout that page has
   always had. Declared `none` here rather than left unstyled so that stays true by rule and not
   by the accident of an empty div being harmless.
   IT MUST PRECEDE THE QUERY: the two declarations have the same (0,1,0) specificity, so the
   later one wins. Written after it, this `none` would beat the `block` inside the query at
   every width and the break would never fire. */
.room-kpi-break {
  display: none;
}

@media (max-width: 1200px) {
  .col-sm-3:has(> .room-kpi) {
    width: 50%;
  }
  /* AND THE LIST PAIR STARTS A LINE OF ITS OWN. At half-width the two list cards can no longer
     share a line with the floated `col-sm-6` action column that precedes them in the markup:
     the float takes half, Goal Words takes the other half, and Curriculum drops to the next
     line by itself -- the stagger this widening introduced.
     The break is a BLOCK box because `clear` has no effect on the inline-block columns
     themselves, so nothing else in the row can reach past that float. Zero height, so the line
     it starts costs no space. Its element lives in templates/organization/room.hbs. */
  .room-kpi-break {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    clear: both;
  }
}
/* WRAPPING. These columns are Bootstrap FLOATS, and floats snag: a column taller than its
   neighbours catches the next one, which then hangs off its side instead of starting a fresh
   row. The six cards are not the same height -- Goal Words and Curriculum carry a list, the
   other four a single line -- so the row staggered as soon as it wrapped.

   `inline-block` instead of `float` is the fix. Inline-block items always begin a new row
   together when they no longer fit, regardless of each other's height, and `vertical-align:
   top` keeps them aligned at the top of that row rather than on their text baselines.
   Widths are untouched, so the 25% / 50% / `col-xs-6` steps still decide how many fit.

   SCOPED TO THE KPI COLUMNS ONLY, via `:has(> .room-kpi)`. The same row holds the action
   buttons and the charts below, which are still floats and still want to be -- mixing the two
   models across the whole row is what would look strange. */
.col-sm-3:has(> .room-kpi) {
  float: none;
  display: inline-block;
  vertical-align: top;
  /* Inline-block honours whitespace between elements; the template indents these onto their
     own lines, which would otherwise add a stray gap to every column's width and drop the
     last card to the next row one step early. */
  margin-right: -4px;
}

/* MORE BREATHING ROOM ABOVE THE ORG HEADER ON LARGE SCREENS (requested 2026-09-23), and more
   again below the switcher when one is on screen.

   ONLY ABOVE 1200px, deliberately. The tight 6px gap this widens was set for the case where
   the switcher sits directly over the title, and on a narrow screen that closeness is what
   keeps the two reading as one block without pushing the content below the fold. Wide screens
   have the height to spare and the tightness reads as cramped instead.
   1201px is the same seam the KPI columns use, so the page has one large/small boundary rather
   than two that disagree.

   TWO VALUES, because the two cases are not the same gap. Without a switcher this is the space
   between the tab strip and the title; with one it is the space under the dropdown, which
   already has its own 28px above it, so it needs slightly less to feel even.
   AT EVERY WIDTH since 2026-10-01 (requested: the page content should not shift as the screen
   narrows): above 1200px only, the title stepped 8px at 1200 (28 -> 20) and 16px with a switcher
   (22 -> 6). These later rules now win at every width, so the 6px rule above no longer applies. */
body.ll-view-basic .md-hero--org {
  padding-top: 28px !important;
}

body.ll-view-basic .md-workspace:has(.ch-org-switcher) .md-hero--org {
  padding-top: 22px !important;
}

/* ORG HEADER ON BASIC VIEW (2026-09-23): no role meta row, and no entrance motion.
   Both are scoped to `body.ll-view-basic`, the app-wide Basic body class, so Modern keeps the
   header exactly as it is.

   THE MOTION RULES CANNOT SIMPLY BE SWITCHED OFF. `.md-hero__title--from-left` and
   `.md-hero__sub--from-right` (app.scss ~34501) each set `opacity: 0` and then animate to
   visible with `forwards` -- the animation is what REVEALS the text, not just what moves it.
   Setting `animation: none` alone would leave the header permanently invisible, so `opacity: 1`
   has to come with it. That is the whole reason this is two declarations and not one.

   No `!important`: the base rules carry none, and `body.ll-view-basic` ahead of the same
   selector is (0,4,0) against their (0,3,0), so these win on specificity alone.

   REPORTS IS EXCLUDED (2026-09-25, requested: "on gentle, add movement to ... the stats page").
   This rule is scoped to `.md-hero--dashboard`, which the REPORTS hero also carries, so stilling
   the org header stilled Reports too -- collateral, not intent: everything above talks about the
   org header and nothing here was ever about the reports dashboard. Excluding it in place, with
   `:not()`, is what restores that page's own entrance motion in Basic; adding a louder rule
   somewhere else to switch it back on would have left this one still claiming Reports.
   ONLY Reports is excluded. Every other Basic hero this reaches -- the org header it was written
   for, and the user dashboard hero -- keeps the stillness it was given.
   GENTLE ONLY WITHOUT A LAYOUT TEST: `body.ll-layout-focused .md-shell--reports-view
   .md-hero--reports-dashboard` is `display: none !important` (_focused-view.scss:1011), so this
   hero does not exist in Focused and cannot animate there. A `:not(.ll-layout-focused)` here
   would be a condition that is already guaranteed.
   THE ROOMS PAGES ARE EXCLUDED TOO (2026-10-01, requested: "on basic view, the rooms page headers
   should show like they do on modern view"), the same way and for the same reason as Reports:
   `md-hero--org-rooms` (controllers/organization.js `roomsSectionActive`) marks the org header on
   the rooms list and a room's page, which then move as Modern's do. In Focused the rooms LIST is
   stilled again, for both views, by the rule in _focused-view.scss that Modern Focused has there. */
body.ll-view-basic .md-hero--dashboard:not(.md-hero--reports-dashboard):not(.md-hero--org-rooms) .md-hero__title .md-hero__title--from-left,
body.ll-view-basic .md-hero--dashboard:not(.md-hero--reports-dashboard):not(.md-hero--org-rooms) .md-hero__sub .md-hero__sub--from-right {
  animation: none;
  opacity: 1;
}

/* The "User · Manager" counts under the org title. Scoped to `.md-hero--org` rather than the
   `--meta` modifier generally, which the reports dashboard also uses and which was not asked
   about. Not on the rooms pages (`md-hero--org-rooms`, 2026-10-01; see the motion rule above):
   they show the counts as Modern does. */
body.ll-view-basic .md-hero--org:not(.md-hero--org-rooms) .md-hero__sub--meta {
  display: none;
}

/* PRIMARY NAV FORM (2026-09-23). `user-pill-nav` swaps its `md-pillnav` classes for these on
   Basic view, so this same strip now serves as the page-level nav that `.md-pillnav--dashboard`
   serves on Modern. The base `.ch-tabs` above is a full-width panel control living inside the
   dashboard's card; as a page nav it should sit centred and only as wide as its items, the way
   the pill row it replaces does.

   THE UPDATES BADGE COMES ACROSS TOO. That pill carries `md-pillnav__badge`, and the swap
   deliberately changes only the BASE class -- `md-pillnav__pill--updates` and the badge are
   modifiers that position the count, and rewriting them would have meant forking the markup
   after all. The badge simply has no `ch-` rule of its own, so it is given the home tabs'
   `.ch-tab__badge` treatment here rather than left unstyled. */
.ch-tabs--primary {
  /* EQUAL-WIDTH TABS, sized to the widest of them (requested 2026-09-23: the same width for
     Home and for the section tab beside it).

     A GRID, not flex, because this is the one thing flex cannot do. `.ch-tab` is `flex: 1 1
     auto`, which grows items from their own content -- so "Home" and "Subscription" end up
     visibly different sizes, which is what prompted this. `grid-auto-columns: 1fr` with a
     `fit-content` track sizes EVERY column to the widest item, so the two match whatever the
     section label happens to be, from "Rooms" to "Subscription", without a hard-coded width
     that would need revisiting each time a section is added.

     The tab's own metrics are untouched -- padding, font and the 44px minimum target all still
     come from `.ch-tab` above -- so these read as home-page tabs, just evenly sized. */
  display: grid;
  grid-auto-flow: column;
  grid-auto-columns: 1fr;
  width: fit-content;
  max-width: 100%;
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
}

/* The grid sizes the track; this centres the label inside it, which flex was doing implicitly
   before and grid does not. */
.ch-tabs--primary .ch-tab {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  text-align: center;
}

/* BREADCRUMB FORM of the strip -- a way back plus the current page, used on the Rooms page.
   Left-aligned rather than the tab row's stretched split: two items sharing the full width
   would read as a choice between equals, and one of them is where you already are. The back
   tab is an <a>, so it needs the anchor colour and underline neutralised to match the buttons
   beside it, which `.ch-tab` alone does not do. */
.ch-tab.is-active {
  background: linear-gradient(160deg, #fff 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86) 100%);
  color: #1B365D;
  box-shadow: 0 3px 8px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.1), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);
}

.ch-tab__badge, .ch-tabs--primary .md-pillnav__badge {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  min-width: 22px;
  height: 22px;
  margin-left: 6px;
  padding: 0 6px;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: #C0392B;
  color: #fff;
  font-size: 14px;
  font-weight: 700;
}

.ch-subtabs {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 5px;
}

.ch-subtab {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: 6px 12px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.12);
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
  color: var(--md-muted, #1B365D);
  font: inherit;
  font-size: 14px;
  font-weight: 600;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background 0.16s ease, border-color 0.16s ease, color 0.16s ease;
}

.ch-subtab:hover {
  background: #fff;
  color: #1B365D;
}

.ch-subtab.is-active {
  background: linear-gradient(160deg, rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.18) 0%, rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.1) 100%);
  border-color: rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.45);
  color: #1B365D;
}

.ch-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(132px, 1fr));
  gap: 12px;
}

.ch-tile {
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  gap: 10px;
  min-height: 108px;
  padding: 16px 12px;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  border-radius: 18px;
  background: linear-gradient(155deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.96) 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.74) 100%);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  box-shadow: 0 6px 18px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.08), 0 1px 3px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.05), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);
  color: #1B365D;
  font: inherit;
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 750;
  letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  line-height: 1.2;
  text-align: center;
  text-decoration: none;
  appearance: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: transform 0.18s ease, box-shadow 0.18s ease;
}

.ch-tile::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0 0 auto 0;
  height: 52%;
  border-radius: inherit;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72) 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.252) 55%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 100%);
  pointer-events: none;
}

.ch-tile::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: -45%;
  left: -25%;
  width: 85%;
  height: 115%;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55) 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 70%);
  pointer-events: none;
}

a.ch-tile:hover, a.ch-tile:focus-visible,
button.ch-tile:hover, button.ch-tile:focus-visible {
  color: #1B365D;
  text-decoration: none;
  transform: translateY(-2px);
  box-shadow: 0 12px 26px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.11), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);
}

.ch-tile__chip {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 48px;
  height: 48px;
  border-radius: 14px;
  background: linear-gradient(160deg, #fff 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86) 100%);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
  box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.1), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);
}

a.ch-tile:hover .ch-tile__chip,
button.ch-tile:hover .ch-tile__chip {
  box-shadow: 0 7px 16px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.14), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95);
}

.ch-tile__icon {
  position: relative;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
}

.ch-tile__label {
  position: relative;
}

.ch-tile__sub,
.ch-tile__rule,
.ch-tile__more {
  display: none;
}

.ch-tile--big .ch-tile__label {
  font-size: clamp(18px, 0.75rem + 0.85vw, 26px);
}

.ch-tile--big {
  --ch-zone-gap: 14px;
  min-height: 124px;
  align-items: stretch;
  justify-content: flex-start;
  text-align: left;
  gap: 0;
}
.ch-tile--big .ch-tile__info {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-width: 0;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
  border-radius: 18px;
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
}
.ch-tile--big .ch-tile__info::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  bottom: calc(-1 * var(--ch-zone-gap) / 2);
  height: 1px;
  background: rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.07);
  box-shadow: 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
  pointer-events: none;
}
.ch-tile--big .ch-tile__text {
  display: flex;
