/* ── BASIC VIEW: ONE MENU HEIGHT ON EVERY PAGE THAT HAS THE RAIL (2026-10-01) ──────────
   Requested: "the menu needs to start at the same height it is on the home page".
   Every Basic rail shell (`.ch-page`, the account section, the org directory, the org pages) insets
   its rail 80px from the top of `#content`, so the rail's height is `#content`'s top padding plus
   that. The home page's `#content` carries `calc(var(--topbar-height) + 1rem)` (app.scss,
   `#content.home-page`) and so do the Focused org pages (_focused-view.scss); the rest fell back to
   the generic `+ 3rem` (app.scss `#within_ember #content`), so their rail sat 20px lower and moved
   as you went from Home to Logs (measured 94 vs 114). Every page that renders the rail takes the
   home page's padding here, so the rail starts at one height everywhere.
   `:has(.ch-rail)` names the condition directly: the rail renders only in Basic, on exactly these
   pages. `!important` matches the page-specific `#content` paddings it sits alongside. */
body.ll-view-basic #within_ember #content:has(.ch-rail) {
  padding-top: calc(var(--topbar-height) + 1rem) !important;
}

/* ── BASIC VIEW: THE ACCOUNT PAGE GETS THE SECTION RAIL BESIDE IT (2026-09-25) ──────────
   Requested: "on basic view account page we need to add the ch-rail to the account page ...
   for focused view, use the basic view focused ch-rail styles; for gentle view, use the basic
   view gentle ch-rail styles".

   THE STYLING IS ALREADY WRITTEN, which is the point of building the rail out of `ch-` classes:
   `.ch-rail` and `.ch-row` carry the glass panel, the rows and both layouts' treatments, so the
   new rail picks up gentle and focused exactly as the dashboard's does. Only the LAYOUT is new,
   and only in Basic -- three declarations, mirroring what `.md-shell--org-directory` does for
   the organizations page (above), which is the same problem solved the same way.

   `:has(> .ch-rail--account)` SCOPES IT TO THE PAGE THAT HAS ONE, and the shell class is
   deliberately NOT part of the test. The rail's presence is the condition: a page that never
   renders it cannot be turned into a row by accident, and a page that gains it needs no new rule.
   That is how this survived the rail following the whole section (2026-09-25) with the qualifier
   as its only edit.

   THE QUALIFIER WAS `.md-shell--user` UNTIL 2026-09-25, and widening it to `.md-shell` is what
   lets Reports in. Reports is the one section page with its OWN shell
   (`.md-shell--reports-view.md-shell--stats`, templates/user/stats.hbs) because `user.stats` is a
   bare route, so it never carried `--user` and every rule here missed it. Specificity is
   unchanged by the swap -- one class out of the compound, one class into the `:has()` argument,
   whose specificity counts -- so nothing this used to outrank it now loses to.

   `--account` RATHER THAN BARE `.ch-rail` IS LOAD-BEARING now that the shell class is gone.
   `templates/organizations.hbs` and the Basic home page render `<Dashboard::ClassicRail />` as a
   direct child of THEIR `.md-shell`, and those rails are plain `.ch-rail`. Testing for the bare
   class would have applied this account-section layout to the dashboard and the org directory.
   `ch-rail--account` is rendered in exactly one place in the app
   (components/dashboard/classic-account-rail.hbs:18), which is what keeps the blast radius at
   this one rail's two mount points.

   MODERN IS NOT TOUCHED: it never renders `.ch-rail--account` at all -- `showClassicAccountRail`
   is false for a non-classic user -- so the `:has()` is false there and every shell keeps its
   column. */
body.ll-view-basic .md-shell:has(> .ch-rail--account) {
  flex-direction: row !important;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 24px;
  /* SIDE INSET ONLY. Without it the rail sits flush against the viewport edge -- this shell
     has no horizontal padding of its own, where `.ch-page` (the Basic home page) carries 28px.
     28 is that same figure, so the rail starts on the same line as the home page's does.
     The VERTICAL padding is deliberately left alone: the account page's top spacing in Basic
     was not part of this request, and the shorthand would have silently reset it. */
  padding-left: 28px !important;
  padding-right: 28px !important;
  /* AND DOWN FROM THE TOP OF THE PAGE (2026-09-25, requested). This shell had only the 30px
     `--ll-nav-clearance`, which put the rail at y=76 with the fixed header's bottom edge at 70
     -- six pixels of daylight under the navbar at 1280, and at 1024, where that clearance is 0,
     the rail started at 46 with its top 24px behind the header.
     80 / 44 / 48 ARE `.ch-page`'S OWN FIGURES at these three widths, measured on the Basic home
     page and the organizations directory: both put their rail at y=126 / 90 / 94. Matching them
     is what makes the three Basic pages that carry this rail start on one line, rather than
     picking a number that only looks right on the page in front of me.
     The content moves down with the rail, keeping the 40px offset it already had between them.
     AT EVERY WIDTH since 2026-10-01: the 44px (<=1024) and 48px (<=900) steps lifted the rail and
     the page 36px at 1024, so they are gone and 80px holds. */
  padding-top: 80px !important;
}

body.ll-view-basic .md-shell:has(> .ch-rail--account) > .md-workspace {
  flex: 1 1 auto !important;
  min-width: 0;
}

/* THE CONTENT COLUMN STARTS WHERE THE RAIL DOES (2026-09-25, requested: "basic view gentle
   style: move the accounts page content up on the page").
   MEASURED at 1280 before the change: rail top 126, workspace top 166 -- the workspace carried a
   40px top MARGIN and a 40px top PADDING, so the content began 80px below the rail beside it and
   the two columns read as unrelated. Both go, which is exactly what
   `.md-shell--org-directory > .md-workspace` above does for the organizations page, for the same
   reason and with the same result: one top edge across the row.
   THE CONTENT KEEPS ITS OWN INSET -- `.md-main--user` has 35px of its own (app.scss), so this
   moves the block up without pushing the card against the shell's edge.
   BOTH LAYOUTS since 2026-10-01 (was Gentle only, as first asked; Focused sat 95px below the
   rail, measured 126 / 221). Holds at every width: the base `.md-workspace` steps its own margin and
   padding at 1024, so zeroing them here is also what keeps the page from moving there. */
body.ll-view-basic .md-shell:has(> .ch-rail--account) > .md-workspace {
  margin-top: 0 !important;
  padding-top: 0 !important;
}

/* ...AND THE CARD'S OWN INSET GOES TOO (2026-09-25, asked for straight after: "move it up more").
   With the workspace at 0 the only box left above the content was `.md-main--user`'s 35px
   (app.scss, `calc(var(--bottombar-height) - 24px)`, matched to its own bottom padding). That
   put the content 35px below the rail beside it, which is the gap that was still visible.
   At 0 the card's top edge and the rail's top edge are the same line -- the alignment the
   organizations directory has, and as far up as this content can go without moving the rail,
   which was deliberately placed at the home page's inset earlier today.
   TOP ONLY: the bottom keeps its 35px, as in the Modern narrow-width rule that does the same
   thing for the same reason. Only where the rail renders; both layouts since 2026-10-01 (above). */
body.ll-view-basic .md-shell:has(> .ch-rail--account) > .md-workspace > .md-main--user {
  padding-top: 0 !important;
}

/* STACKS WHERE EVERY OTHER BASIC RAIL STACKS. At 900 and below `.ch-rail` is already
   `max-width: none; width: 100%` and is built to sit ABOVE the content, not beside it (see the
   note at the collapse control) -- left as a row it would be a full-width panel squeezing the
   account card into whatever was left. Same breakpoint and same reason as `.ch-page` and the
   organizations directory. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  body.ll-view-basic .md-shell:has(> .ch-rail--account) {
    flex-direction: column !important;
    /* It still clears the fixed header: the 80px above holds here too (2026-10-01; it was 48px,
       which put the stacked rail 32px higher than the side rail). */
  }
}
/* Sides narrow with `.ch-page`'s at the same width, for the same reason. */
@media (max-width: 767px) {
  body.ll-view-basic .md-shell:has(> .ch-rail--account) {
    padding-left: 22px !important;
    padding-right: 22px !important;
  }
}
/* THE CURRENT PAGE'S ROW, which the dashboard rail never needed. Its rows are actions, so none
   of them is ever "where you are"; every row here is a page, and one of them always is.
   BORDER AND WEIGHT, NOT A FILL: the rows sit on a glass panel whose contrast budget is
   already spent, and darkening one row enough to read as selected against it pushed the label
   below the section's own contrast floor. A heavier left edge in the brand teal plus the
   title's weight says "here" without touching the text colour. */
/* ── THE ACCOUNT RAIL'S ROWS SIT CLOSER AT 1024 AND BELOW (2026-09-25) ──────────────────
   Requested: "on 1024px basic view reduce the spacing between the buttons on the ch-rail for
   the account page".

   IT WAS TWO DIFFERENT GAPS, which is why this is two declarations. Measured on the account
   rail at 1024: Gentle 6px, Focused 12px. The 6 is `.ch-rail__list`'s own `gap`; Focused adds
   another 6 through `.ch-rail__item + .ch-rail__item { padding-top: 6px }` (_focused-view.scss,
   in a `min-width: 901px` block), which that rule's note calls "half of the 12px rhythm". So a
   single `gap` change would have tightened one layout and left the other reading loose.
   BOTH LAND ON 4px, one number rather than two proportional ones: the rows are 60px tall at
   this width and the two layouts otherwise sit 6px apart on a spacing nobody can see a reason
   for. Gentle gives up 2px, Focused 8px, and the rail reads the same density in both.

   SCOPED TO `.ch-rail--account`, as asked -- the dashboard and organizations rails keep the
   spacing they have. The Focused half is written here rather than beside its rule so the two
   halves of one change stay together; it is the same selector as that rule plus this rail's
   class, so it outranks it (0,4,0 against 0,3,0) inside this narrower range.
   <=1024 INCLUDES the grid layout below 901px, where `gap` governs both axes and the rows sit
   in columns -- the same 4px applies there and reads consistently. */
@media (max-width: 1024px) {
  .ch-rail--account .ch-rail__list {
    gap: 4px;
  }
  body.ll-layout-focused .ch-rail--account .ch-rail__item + .ch-rail__item {
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  /* THE BASIC HOME (dashboard) RAIL TIGHTENS TOO (requested 2026-10-13): reduce its row gap by 4px
     at 1024 and below, on the COLLAPSED rail only (the state it auto-collapses into at <=1024).
     Base is 6px (.ch-rail__list:914), so 2px here. `.ch-rail.ch-rail--collapsed` per the specificity
     note at :458; scoped away from the account and org rails, which carry their own 4px rule above. */
  .ch-rail.ch-rail--collapsed:not(.ch-rail--account):not(.ch-rail--org) .ch-rail__list {
    gap: 2px;
  }
}
/* THE ORG RAIL SAYS "YOU ARE HERE" THE SAME WAY (2026-09-25, requested: "Show Organizations as
   the active button we are on"). Listed with the account rail's rather than given its own rule:
   they are two instances of one idea, and `.ch-row.is-active` unqualified would also reach the
   Basic dashboard's rail, whose rows are ACTIONS -- none of them is ever a page you are on. */
.ch-rail--account .ch-row.is-active,
.ch-rail--org .ch-row.is-active {
  border-left: 4px solid #2A9D8F;
}

/* BOTH LABELS, because which one is on screen depends on the rail's state. `.ch-row__title`
   lives inside `.ch-row__body`, and `.ch-row__body` is CLIPPED in the collapsed rail (:446) --
   so the weight half of this treatment was invisible exactly when the rail is narrowest and the
   4px edge is the only other signal. `.ch-row__short` is the label that shows there (:471).
   THIS IS NOT A HYPOTHETICAL STATE: `classic-account-rail.js` auto-collapses the rail at 1024
   and below, and it shares its stashed collapse state with the dashboard rail, so someone who
   collapsed that one on the home page walks into this section collapsed.
   `.ch-row__short`'s own `font-weight: 650` is (0,2,0); this is (0,3,0) and wins. The expanded
   rail is unaffected -- `.ch-row__short` is `display: none` there (:396). */
.ch-rail--account .ch-row.is-active .ch-row__title,
.ch-rail--account .ch-row.is-active .ch-row__short,
.ch-rail--org .ch-row.is-active .ch-row__title,
.ch-rail--org .ch-row.is-active .ch-row__short {
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* ── BASIC VIEW: THE RAIL BESIDE A SELECTED ORGANISATION (2026-09-25, requested) ────────
   `templates/organization.hbs` mounts the rail in org mode; this is the layout that puts it
   beside the page instead of above it, and it is the same three-part shape the account rail and
   the org DIRECTORY already use: turn the shell into a row, let the workspace take the rest, and
   inset the pair from the viewport edge.

   `:not(.md-shell--org-directory)` KEEPS THE TWO APART. Both pages carry `.md-shell--org`, and
   the directory already has its own block further up this file with the SAME row treatment plus
   two things this page must not take: it zeroes the workspace's top margin AND padding to pull
   "My Organizations" level with the rail. The org detail pages have a different header
   (`.md-hero--org`, whose top padding was widened on large screens by an earlier request), so
   borrowing that would have moved content nobody asked to move. Hence a sibling block rather
   than adding a selector to theirs -- the layout is shared, the vertical trim is not.

   THE RAIL'S OWN INSET IS THE SAME 80 / 44 / 48 the other two Basic rails use, so all three
   pages start their rail on one line. */
body.ll-view-basic .md-shell--org:not(.md-shell--org-directory) {
  flex-direction: row !important;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 24px;
  padding-left: 28px !important;
  padding-right: 28px !important;
  padding-top: 80px !important;
}

body.ll-view-basic .md-shell--org:not(.md-shell--org-directory) > .md-workspace {
  flex: 1 1 auto !important;
  min-width: 0;
}

/* ORG PAGES: NO TOP GAP, LEVEL WITH THE RAIL, AT EVERY WIDTH THE RAIL SITS BESIDE THEM.
   History: 2026-09-28 the Focused Rooms list only (requested: "remove the top margin and top
   padding" from the workspace); 2026-10-01 the rooms section in both layouts, then every org page
   (requested: content should not shift at 1024px and below, and should sit level with the top of
   the side menu -- "the alignment option").
   BOTH VALUES come from the base `.md-workspace` (`margin: 40px auto; padding: 40px ...`, both
   `!important`, app.scss), which is why this matches them with `!important` -- the same
   arrangement the org directory's block above uses for the same two boxes. That base also drops
   its margin to 0 and steps its padding 40 -> 52 -> 40 at 1024 and below, which is what moved
   these pages there (measured, Focused: rail 106 -> 70, page 186 -> 150; Gentle: rail 126 -> 90,
   page 206 -> 130 / 142); zeroing both here holds the page still across those steps, and the
   shell's 80px (above) holds the rail.
   ABOVE 900px only: at 900 and below the rail stacks over the page, and where the content lands
   under it is a layout change, not this gap. `margin-top` / `padding-top` longhands, so the sides
   and bottom are untouched. The page's own header keeps its inset (`.md-hero--org`'s padding). */
@media (min-width: 901px) {
  body.ll-view-basic .md-shell--org:not(.md-shell--org-directory) > .md-workspace {
    margin-top: 0 !important;
    padding-top: 0 !important;
  }
}
/* Stacks where every other Basic rail stacks: at 900 and below `.ch-rail` is already full-width
   and built to sit ABOVE the content, so left as a row it would squeeze the org page into
   whatever was left. Same breakpoint as the others; the 48px header clearance they take is not
   taken here (2026-10-01, see the rule). */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  body.ll-view-basic .md-shell--org:not(.md-shell--org-directory) {
    flex-direction: column !important;
    /* No 48px top padding here any more (2026-10-01, the no-shift request above): the shell keeps
       its 80px, so the stacked rail starts where the side rail did. */
  }
}
@media (max-width: 767px) {
  body.ll-view-basic .md-shell--org:not(.md-shell--org-directory) {
    padding-left: 22px !important;
    padding-right: 22px !important;
  }
}
