/* GLASSMORPHISM, board-alt speak bar (requested).
   THE GLASS IS ON `#inner_header` -- background, blur and sheen all on the one element, which
   is where the bar's colour has always lived.

   The outer `header.speaking` is therefore TRANSPARENT rather than charcoal. That is not a
   spare change: `#inner_header` is its child, so an opaque parent is the only thing the
   child's `backdrop-filter` would ever see, and the blur would frost a flat colour and render
   as an ordinary solid bar. Clearing the parent lets the glass see the PAGE -- the header is
   `position: fixed`, so board content scrolls underneath it and there is something real to
   frost. `#inner_header` fills the header box (no padding or margin on `header.speaking` in
   this state, checked), so nothing shows through around it.

   SAFE TO PUT A BACKDROP-FILTER HERE, in this state, and it was checked rather than assumed:
   `backdrop-filter` makes an element the containing block for every `position: fixed`
   descendant, and the compiled CSS has NINE rules fixing `#inner_header` descendants
   (#header_search, the mobile auth nav, the support button, the create-board link). All nine
   need `.app-navbar` or `#nav_header` inside it, and
   `controllers/application.js#useAppNavbarInHeader` returns FALSE for `user.board-alt.index`,
   so board-alt never renders the navbar and this selector's `:has(#speak)` state renders the
   speak bar instead. If a future change puts the navbar back into board-alt's header, this
   blur must move to a pseudo-element first.

   ALPHA IS MEASURED, NOT EYEBALLED. The bar carries white text. On plain charcoal that is
   8.16:1; translucency erodes it, and the WORST case is the bar scrolled over white content.
   At 0.82 -- the value that looks right -- white text falls to 4.11:1 and FAILS AA. At 0.92 it
   holds 5.15:1 in that worst case and 6.22:1 over ordinary dark content. Not headroom an AAC
   surface should trade for a finish. The 0.10 white film is counted in both figures. */
#within_ember.board-alt-view header.speaking:has(#speak) {
  background: transparent !important;
}

#within_ember.board-alt-view header.speaking:has(#speak) #inner_header {
  /* Darkened in step with the non-speak bar above (same 28%-toward-black mix and the same
     halved sheen) so the two board-alt header states do not drift apart. Alpha can relax
     0.92 -> 0.86 here precisely BECAUSE the base is darker now: the 0.92 floor existed to
     protect white text on the lighter $brand-charcoal-blue, and that constraint moves with
     the colour. */
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05) 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02) 45%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.01) 100%), rgba(50.4, 57.6, 68.4, 0.86) !important;
  backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(1.3);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(1.3);
  /* The glass EDGE, not the light border this rule used to strip. The old `border-bottom:
     none` existed because the base `#inner_header:has(#speak)` rule leaks a pale border meant
     for light speak pages, which showed as a white sliver against the dark surround. This is a
     deliberate translucent-white hairline, which is what gives a glass panel its edge. */
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14) !important;
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2), 0 6px 18px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.16) !important;
}

