/* Applied in: speak-menu component - speak options dropdown */
/* ── Modern Speak Menu ─────────────────────────────────── */
.modal-content:has(.md-speak-menu) {
  border-radius: 20px;
  border: none;
  overflow: hidden;
  background: #ffffff;
  /* THIS is the rule that stops the bottom being cut off — not the max-height on
     .md-speak-menu below.

     modal-dialog.js:97 runs `$(el).find('.modal-content').css('maxHeight',
     $(window).height() - 50)` for EVERY modal in the app, as an INLINE style. Inline
     beats any stylesheet rule, so .md-speak-menu's own `100vh - --sm-menu-top - 16px`
     was being applied inside a parent capped 30px tighter (540 vs 510 at a 560px
     viewport, measured). The menu was therefore taller than its own container and the
     overflow was clipped HERE, one level up from every rule that kept being edited to
     fix it.

     Same override, same reason, as `.modal-content:has(.la-find-button-wrap)` — whose
     comment names this same line of modal-dialog.js. Neutralising the global cap lets
     the menu's own max-height govern, which already accounts for the 4px top offset
     speak-menu.js sets and leaves 16px below the fold.

     Verified with scripts/speak-menu-clip-qa.mjs, not by reading the cascade. */
  max-height: none !important;
}

.md-speak-menu {
  padding: 16px 20px 12px !important;
  font-size: 16px;
  background: #ffffff;
  border-radius: 20px;
  /* Never taller than the room left below the sentence bar, and SCROLLS rather than
     clipping when it would be.

     This used to be a bare `overflow: hidden` with no max-height, which meant the menu
     grew to whatever its content needed and everything past the bottom of the screen was
     simply cut off — unreachable, with nothing on screen to say so. Short viewports and
     the larger vocalization-size settings (which push the menu down by up to 160px, see
     --sm-menu-top) hit it first.

     `--sm-menu-top` is set by speak-menu.js#opening when it positions the dialog, so this
     subtracts the real offset rather than a second guess at it; the fallback matches the
     default 100px vocalization height. The x axis stays clipped for the 20px radius —
     `hidden` first so Safari < 16 keeps the old behaviour, then `clip`, because `hidden`
     on one axis computes the other to `auto` and would make this a scroll container in
     both directions. */
  max-height: calc(100vh - var(--sm-menu-top, 4px) - 16px);
  overflow-x: hidden;
  overflow-x: clip;
  overflow-y: auto;
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}

/* Header */
.md-speak-menu__header {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  /* STICKY, so the title, the close control and the Up/Down scroll controls stay on screen
     however far the menu is scrolled -- the scroll controls are useless if scrolling can
     carry them away.

     The negative margins cancel `.md-speak-menu`'s own `padding: 16px 20px 12px` and the
     matching padding puts the same space back inside the bar. Without that the bar would
     sit inside the padding box and scrolled content would show through the 20px channel
     either side of it and the 16px above. Opaque background for the same reason: sticky
     only reserves space, it does not hide what passes underneath.

     The BOTTOM margin (third value) is the gap between the divider and whatever section
     leads the view. It was 14px, which left the first section label sitting almost on the
     rule. Raised here rather than padding the first section, because every view leads with
     a different one -- Punctuation on the main view, Repeat Words under Repeats -- and the
     spacing belongs to the header, not to whichever section happens to be first. */
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  z-index: 3;
  background: #ffffff;
  margin: -16px -20px 24px;
  padding: 16px 20px 12px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.15);
}

/* Groups the scroll controls with the close button so the header's `space-between` keeps
   the title left and every control right, however many of them are currently rendered. */
/* ABSOLUTELY CENTRED on the header, for two reasons. It reads as centred regardless of how
   wide the title or the close button are, and -- the functional half -- taking it out of
   flow means the pair appearing or disappearing cannot move the title or the close button.
   The header is `position: sticky`, which already establishes the containing block. */
.md-speak-menu__scroll-pager {
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%;
  top: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
}

/* No rule of its own: `.md-speak-menu__scroll-btn` is applied ALONGSIDE
   `.md-speak-menu__phrase-page-btn` in the template, so both pagers are governed by that one
   declaration block (~:18900) and cannot drift apart. This class exists only as a hook --
   it is what modal.js#scannable_targets matches to make these reachable by switch scanning.
   Do not give it competing declarations here; edit the shared rule instead. */
.md-speak-menu__title {
  /* 22px. Was `1.25rem` with a comment claiming 20px — but the root font-size in this
     codebase is 10px, set by bootstrap on the html element and measured in the browser, so
     it rendered at 12.5px: a modal title smaller than the body text under it. px, so the
     number in the rule is the number on screen.

     NOTE — no braces in this comment, deliberately. A `{ }` pair inside a comment here
     silently swallowed the whole declaration block: the rule reached the served CSS intact
     and the browser applied none of it, leaving the h2 at its 25px user-agent default.
     Verified by removing them. Keep brace-free. */
  /* Fluid 14-22px, and `!important` is not a shortcut here — it is the only way to win.
     The bare element rule `h2` at app.scss:3614 declares `font-size: 25px !important`, so
     this class rule was losing and the title rendered at 25px, NOT the 22px above it. An
     important class selector 0,1,0 beats an important element selector 0,0,1; nothing
     without !important can. Scoping the global h2 rule instead would reach every h2 in the
     app, which is not this modal's call to make.
     Bootstrap is not the culprit, despite appearances in devtools: its `.h2, h2 30px` and
     `body 14px` sit BELOW the app rule in the cascade and were already being overridden.

     No arithmetic inside the clamp, deliberately: dart-sass strips a calc() wrapper inside
     a range function and emits bare mixed-unit math, which is what the CSS/SCSS guideline
     says SassC cannot evaluate. Three plain values avoid the question. 2.45vw reaches the
     22px ceiling at ~900px — the width above which this modal stops filling the screen —
     and bottoms out at the 14px floor around 571px — phone widths sit well below that
     crossover, so the title resolves to a flat 14px on every phone. The 14px floor is
     deliberate and sits below the app's usual minimum; do not raise it back to match the
     surrounding body copy. */
  font-size: clamp(14px, 2.45vw, 22px) !important;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: #1B365D;
}

.md-speak-menu__close {
  width: 44px; /* WCAG 2.5.5 — minimum touch target 44×44 */
  height: 44px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.12);
  background: rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.05);
  border-radius: 50%;
  cursor: pointer;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  color: #6B7280;
  transition: background 0.12s ease, color 0.12s ease, transform 0.12s ease;
  padding: 0;
}

.md-speak-menu__close:hover {
  background: rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.1);
  color: #374151;
  transform: scale(1.05);
}

/* Sections */
.md-speak-menu__section {
  margin-bottom: 20px;
}

.md-speak-menu__section--punctuation {
  /* Punctuation is the FIRST section now — separator sits UNDERNEATH it. */
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.15);
  padding-bottom: 16px;
}

.md-speak-menu__section-label {
  /* 14px FLOOR, and nothing in this file reduces it — no responsive rule targets this
     selector, so 14px is what every viewport gets.

     Was `1rem` with a comment claiming 16px; at this codebase's 10px root that rendered
     at 10px, uppercase and letter-spaced, which is where a section heading stops being
     legible for the people this product is built for. Same trap as the title above. */
  font-size: 14px;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.85); /* ~7.8:1 contrast on white — passes AAA */
  margin-bottom: 8px;
  padding-left: 2px;
}

/* Button rows */
.md-speak-menu__row {
  display: grid;
  gap: 8px;
}

.md-speak-menu__row--1 {
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
}

.md-speak-menu__row--2 {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
}

.md-speak-menu__row--3 {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
}

/* Actions: Share, Repeats, Repairs, Alerts, Hold Thought. Wraps to 3 across on a
   phone in the media block below — 5 across is not a reliable touch target at that
   width, the same judgement --3 and --7 already carry. */
.md-speak-menu__row--5 {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
}

/* Full punctuation set in one row (single-tap reach, no "More" submenu). 7 across
   only while the targets stay comfortably hittable — see the narrow-screen wrap below. */
.md-speak-menu__row--7 {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(7, 1fr);
}

.md-speak-menu__row--locale {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(148px, 1fr));
}

/* Unread-alerts count on the Tools > Alerts button.

   Same two signals the navbar's speak-options badge uses
   (templates/application.hbs — `referenced_user.unread_alerts`, then
   `request_alert` as a bare `*`), so the two cannot disagree about whether there
   is something waiting. Written as a class here rather than the inline `style=`
   that one carries; the button is `position: relative` for it to anchor to. */
.md-speak-menu__btn--badged {
  position: relative;
}

/* Speak menu: board tools (speak mode + languages) above main actions */
.md-speak-menu__section-wrap--board-tools {
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.15);
  margin-bottom: 16px;
  padding-bottom: 4px;
}

/* No separator beneath this wrap — used on the Speak Mode section (the LAST
   section), so there's no divider line trailing the bottom of the modal. */
.md-speak-menu__section-wrap--no-divider {
  border-bottom: none;
  margin-bottom: 0;
  padding-bottom: 0;
}

.md-speak-menu__btn--text-row {
  min-height: 52px;
  justify-content: center;
  padding: 12px 10px;
}

.md-speak-menu__btn--locale-chip {
  min-height: 48px;
  padding: 10px 8px;
}

.md-speak-menu__btn-label--locale {
  font-size: 1rem; /* 16px — meets WCAG AA body text, bold compensates for slightly under 18px */
  line-height: 1.35;
  font-weight: 600;
  word-break: break-word;
}

/* Individual buttons */
.md-speak-menu__btn {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  gap: 6px;
  padding: 14px 8px;
  cursor: pointer;
  text-align: center;
  position: relative;
  border-radius: 14px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.08);
  background: #E9EBED;
  transition: background 0.12s ease, box-shadow 0.12s ease;
  color: #1B365D;
  box-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06);
}

.md-speak-menu__btn:hover {
  background: #F2F3F5;
  box-shadow: 0 3px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}

.md-speak-menu__btn:active {
  background: #DFE1E4;
  transform: scale(0.97);
}

.md-speak-menu__btn svg {
  flex-shrink: 0;
  /* Flat duotone base: every icon's primary shapes are AA-contrast teal; accent
     paths set their own stroke="#46505F" (charcoal) inline. Uses the AA token
     ($brand-verdigris-aa, 4.73:1 on white) rather than the dashboard's #2A9D8F
     so the icons clear WCAG 1.4.11 on the grey speak-menu button background.
     (Per-path stroke attrs win over this inherited value.) */
  stroke: #1A7B7A;
}

.md-speak-menu__btn-label {
  /* 14px FLOOR — the label under every button in this modal: Actions (Share, Repeats,
     Repairs, Alerts, Hold Thought) and Phrases.

     Was `1.125rem` with a comment claiming "18px — AAC requirement". At this codebase's
     10px root that rendered at 11.25px, so the requirement the comment names was never
     actually met by the rule that names it. 14px, in px, is met.

     Not 18px, tempting as the old comment is: at 480px the punctuation row is still 7
     across and its buttons are 47px wide, and an 18px label does not fit one. If 18px is
     wanted, the 460px wrap has to move up to ~520px first — a separate decision about
     where the columns break, not a font change. */
  font-size: 14px;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.35;
  color: #374151;
}

/* Unread-alerts count on the Alerts button, and the `*` for a pending request. Reads the
   same two signals as the navbar's speak-options badge (templates/application.hbs) so the
   two cannot disagree about whether something is waiting.

   `min-width` + `text-align` so a two-digit count and a single `*` are the same shape
   rather than one being a disc and the other a stripe. White on #C0392B is 5.44:1, which
   clears WCAG 1.4.3 AA for normal-size text — the bar 14px bold has to meet, being below
   the 18.66px where the large-text 3:1 allowance starts. */
.md-speak-menu__badge {
  position: absolute;
  top: 4px;
  right: 6px;
  min-width: 22px;
  background: #C0392B;
  color: #fff;
  font-size: 14px;
  font-weight: 700;
  padding: 2px 6px;
  border-radius: 10px;
  line-height: 1;
  text-align: center;
}

/* -20% (2026-08-27, Traci): the marks were visually dominating the modal's first
   row. 42 -> 33.6 and 36 -> 28.8, rounded to the nearest whole pixel. Line-height is
   scaled with the size rather than left at 36px, which would have opened a gap under
   each glyph and undone the size change visually. */
.md-speak-menu__punctuation {
  font-size: 34px;
  line-height: 29px;
  display: block;
  color: #374151;
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* The contraction button's WORD label (e.g. "nursing home's"), not a glyph. Same -20%
   for consistency across the row: 22 -> 17.6 and 28 -> 22.4, rounded. 18px still sits
   above a typical web default, so the AAC size floor holds — but this is the one value
   in the row where shrinking costs real legibility, so revert this rule alone if it
   reads small on a device. */
.md-speak-menu__punctuation--sm {
  font-size: 18px;
  line-height: 22px;
}

#menu_punctuation_button .md-speak-menu__punctuation--sm {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #2A9D8F, #4C86D8);
  -webkit-background-clip: text;
  -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
  background-clip: text;
}

/* Bottom actions */
.md-speak-menu__bottom {
  padding-top: 8px;
  margin-top: 4px;
}

/* Footer row: Save Phrase on the left, volume right-aligned on the SAME row. Volume moved here
   out of the Repeats group so it no longer costs an extra activation to reach.
   `.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn` is `width: 100%` by default (see below), which would push the
   volume group onto its own line — hence `width: auto` + `flex` here rather than a fixed width.
   The row is rendered even when Save Phrase is not (it needs a composed sentence), so volume
   stays reachable with an empty sentence bar; `margin-left: auto` keeps it right-aligned in
   both cases. */
.md-speak-menu__bottom-row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-row .md-speak-menu__bottom-btn {
  width: auto;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-volume {
  display: flex;
  gap: 8px;
  margin-left: auto;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Compact, horizontal variant of the tile for this row. The default `.md-speak-menu__btn` is a
   tall COLUMN tile (icon over label) sized for the main grid, which would dominate the footer.
   Scoped to this container rather than adding a modifier class, so the moved markup keeps its
   ids and classes exactly as they were in the Repeats group. */
.md-speak-menu__bottom-volume .md-speak-menu__btn {
  flex-direction: row;
  gap: 6px;
  padding: 8px 12px;
  border-radius: 10px;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 8px;
  width: 100%;
  padding: 10px 12px;
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 10px;
  cursor: pointer;
  font-size: 15px;
  font-weight: 500;
  color: #1B365D;
  transition: background 0.12s ease;
  text-align: left;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn:hover {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--remember {
  color: #2A9D8F;
  font-weight: 600;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--action {
  /* WCAG AA fix — was $brand-dusty-denim on white = 3.49:1. */
  color: #2860C9;
  font-weight: 600;
}

/* The saved/stashed phrase rows, listed INSIDE the Phrases section rather than loose in
   the bottom bar. The indent and hairline are what say "these belong to the button above";
   without them the rows sat flush with the modal edge and read as a separate, unlabelled
   list.

   HEIGHT-CAPPED as a backstop. The list is composed to hold at most three rows — the
   parked entries plus one saved phrase, see speak-menu.js#opening — so this never bites
   today; it is here so the panel cannot grow without limit if that composition changes.
   `--sm-phrase-row` is the row height, matching the min-height the rows carry below.

   The gradients are the classic background-attachment: local scroll shadow: the white pair
   are painted with the CONTENT so they slide away at the top and bottom of the scroll,
   while the grey pair are painted against the SCROLLPORT so they stay put — the net effect
   is a shadow that appears only on the edge you can still scroll towards. No listener, and
   nothing to tear down. It is a supplement to the text hint below the list, never the only
   cue: an eye-gaze or switch user may never resolve a soft gradient at all. */
.md-speak-menu__phrase-list {
  --sm-phrase-rows: 4;
  --sm-phrase-row: 44px;
  margin-top: 8px;
  padding-left: 12px;
  /* Room on the right for the scrollbar AND for the rows' own border and shadow. The rows
     are `width: 100%`, so with no right padding they ran to the content edge and the
     scrollbar sat on top of them — the cards looked sliced off down that side.
     `scrollbar-gutter: stable` reserves the channel even when the list is short enough not
     to scroll, so the rows do not change width the moment a sixth phrase is added; a list
     that reflows as it grows is disorienting on a surface someone scans. */
  padding-right: 10px;
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;
  border-left: 2px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.12);
  /* PAGED, not scrolled — and therefore NO max-height and NO overflow at all.
     speak-menu.js slices the list to PHRASE_PAGE_SIZE rows, so the ROW COUNT is what is
     bounded. An earlier version also capped the height at
     `--sm-phrase-rows * --sm-phrase-row`, which assumed a fixed 44px row; the rows are
     `min-height: 44px` and GROW when a phrase wraps, so four long phrases exceeded the cap
     and were clipped (measured: listScrolls true on three of four pages). Height is bounded
     instead by the row count plus the two-line clamp on the phrase text below — the same
     fix the contacts grid needed. --sm-phrase-row is retained only as the row FLOOR. */
  background: linear-gradient(#fff 30%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0)) top/100% 20px no-repeat local, linear-gradient(rgba(255, 255, 255, 0), #fff 70%) bottom/100% 20px no-repeat local, radial-gradient(farthest-side at 50% 0, rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.13), rgba(27, 54, 93, 0)) top/100% 8px no-repeat scroll, radial-gradient(farthest-side at 50% 100%, rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.13), rgba(27, 54, 93, 0)) bottom/100% 8px no-repeat scroll;
}

/* Each phrase reads as its own pressable card rather than a line of text. The base
   .md-speak-menu__bottom-btn is a flat transparent row, which was right when these sat in
   the bottom bar among Send Reply and Un-Flip Text; inside a list of things you TAP TO SAY
   they need an edge, a surface and a press.

   `min-height: 44px` is the WCAG 2.5.5 AAA target size, and it is what makes five rows a
   predictable 220px — worth having on a control an AAC user aims at with a head pointer. */
.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--remembered {
  /* FIXED height, not a min-height. speak-menu.js#recompute_phrase_page_size divides the
     available space by the row pitch to decide how many phrases fit on screen, and that
     arithmetic is only exact if every row is the same height. 60px (border-box) leaves a
     40px content box, which holds the two lines the phrase text is clamped to at
     16px/1.2 = 38.4px. Change either number and PHRASE_ROW_H in speak-menu.js has to
     follow — they are two halves of one calculation. */
  height: 60px;
  margin-bottom: 6px;
  padding: 9px 14px;
  font-size: 14px;
  color: #374151;
  text-align: left;
  background: #fff;
  border: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.14);
  border-radius: 12px;
  box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.06);
  transition: background-color 0.12s ease, border-color 0.12s ease, box-shadow 0.12s ease;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--remembered:last-child {
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--remembered:hover {
  background: rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.07);
  border-color: rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.4);
}

/* The press itself. Kept to colour and shadow rather than a transform — this list is used
   by people driving a pointer they cannot hold perfectly still, and a row that moves under
   the cursor is a row that can slip out from under it. */
.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--remembered:active {
  background: rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.14);
  box-shadow: none;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--remembered:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid #2A9D8F;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* RESUME — the parked Hold Thought, one step up the hierarchy from the saved phrases it
   sits above. It is the row with something at stake: unfinished, and gone if not picked up.
   Verdigris accent edge + tint rather than a bigger font, so it reads as a different KIND
   of row at a glance without making the list ragged. */
.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--resume {
  border-color: rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.55);
  border-left: 4px solid #2A9D8F;
  background: rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.06);
  box-shadow: 0 1px 3px rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.18);
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--resume:hover {
  background: rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.13);
  border-color: rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.7);
  border-left-color: #2A9D8F;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--resume svg {
  opacity: 1;
  color: #1A7B7A;
}

/* The "Resume:" / "Say:" label. Was bare text between the icon and the phrase; naming it
   lets Resume carry the weight without touching the phrase itself, which stays the loudest
   thing in the row in both cases — it is what the user is actually choosing between. */
.md-speak-menu__remembered-kind {
  flex-shrink: 0;
  font-weight: 600;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--resume .md-speak-menu__remembered-kind {
  color: #1A7B7A;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: 0.01em;
}

/* HELD THOUGHTS — a sub-heading INSIDE the Actions section, under the row of tiles, so it
   needs separation above it that a section label at the top of its own section does not.
   Reuses .md-speak-menu__section-label for the type treatment rather than restating it;
   this modifier only adds the gap. */
.md-speak-menu__held-label {
  margin-top: 14px;
}

/* Reuses .md-speak-menu__phrase-list for the rail and spacing. Unlike the phrase library
   it never pages -- it shows a short window and expands in place -- so it needs no row
   budget, and the `--sm-phrase-rows` custom property it inherits is simply unused here. */
.md-speak-menu__held-list {
  margin-top: 6px;
}

/* The overflow control, on the same glass-tile primitive as the GIF / Share / Alerts action
   tiles (`.la-gif-modal__action` ~:106638 and `.la-inbox__action`): white gradient over a
   tint, backdrop blur, and a four-layer shadow with a lit top edge. Reused rather than
   re-specified so depth and hover read identically wherever the treatment appears.

   Deliberately NOT `--resume`: it is a disclosure for the rest of the list, not a thought to
   pick up, so it must not carry the verdigris accent that marks a real held thought — a row
   that resumes nothing should not look like three that do. The glass tile separates it by
   MATERIAL instead of by colour, which keeps that distinction without a dashed edge. */
.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--more-thoughts {
  justify-content: center;
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.98);
  --tile-tint: #E8ECF1;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95) 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82) 45%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72) 100%), var(--tile-tint);
  backdrop-filter: blur(10px) saturate(140%);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(10px) saturate(140%);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.07), 0 2px 4px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.1), 0 12px 30px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.18), inset 0 1px 0 rgb(255, 255, 255);
  transition: background 0.15s ease, border-color 0.15s ease, box-shadow 0.15s ease, transform 0.15s ease;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--more-thoughts:hover {
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9) 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72) 45%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6) 100%), var(--tile-tint);
  border-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
  transform: translateY(-1px);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.1), 0 3px 6px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.12), 0 18px 40px rgba(17, 26, 46, 0.22), inset 0 1px 0 rgb(255, 255, 255);
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--more-thoughts:active {
  transform: translateY(1px);
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--more-thoughts .md-speak-menu__remembered-text {
  font-weight: 700;
  color: rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.85); /* same ~7.8:1 as the section label */
  flex: 0 0 auto; /* centred with the chevron, not stretched like a phrase row's text */
}

/* Chevron points DOWN to expand and flips UP once expanded, so the control reads as a
   disclosure rather than a link to somewhere else. Paired with `aria-expanded` on the
   button — the rotation is the sighted half of the same signal. */
.md-speak-menu__more-thoughts-chevron {
  flex-shrink: 0;
  transition: transform 0.15s ease;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--more-thoughts-open .md-speak-menu__more-thoughts-chevron {
  transform: rotate(180deg);
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .md-speak-menu__more-thoughts-chevron {
    transition: none;
  }
}
/* "3 more — scroll to see them", under the scroller. */
/* `.md-speak-menu__phrase-list--expanded` used to drop the row cap here and let the panel
   grow, falling back on .md-speak-menu's own scroll past the viewport. Removed: the
   expanded library is PAGED now, so one page is always exactly one page tall and no scroll
   region is created. See the pager rules below and the comment on `rememberedUtterances`
   in speak-menu.js. */
/* Up / Down pager under the phrase list. 44px targets (WCAG 2.5.5) because these are aimed
   at with a head pointer or eye gaze, not a mouse. */
.md-speak-menu__phrase-pager {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: 8px;
  margin-top: 6px;
  padding-left: 12px;
}

/* The Board Actions modal's Up/Down pager is listed here rather than given a rule of its
   own, for the same reason `.md-speak-menu__scroll-btn` has no separate declarations: one
   block governs every pager in the app so they cannot drift apart. Only POSITIONING
   differs between them, and that lives on each modal's own `__scroll-pager` wrapper. */
/* GLASS SURFACE from the shared `ch-chip` recipe (_chips.scss) rather than the flat
   translucent-white fill this carried, so the pagers match the app's raised-glass objects.
   The mixin supplies background, border and box-shadow only — radius, size and type stay
   here, which is the split it documents ("same material, different object").

   INK IS `$la-navy`, NOT `$brand-slate-blue`. The chip is a silver ramp, not near-white:
   slate blue (#5A6A85) measures 3.88:1 on its darkest stop (#D2DAE5), under the 4.5:1 a
   14px/700 label owes — the flat white fill it used to sit on was hiding that. `$la-navy`
   measures 8.60:1 there, and `$indigo` on hover 10.10:1. */
.md-speak-menu__phrase-page-btn,
.la-board-actions__scroll-btn {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  gap: 5px;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: 6px 16px;
  border-radius: 12px;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9) 0%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.42) 48%, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 64%), linear-gradient(160deg, #EAEFF6 0%, #D2DAE5 100%);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95), inset 0 -2px 4px rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.1), 0 3px 8px rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.18);
  color: #1B365D;
  font-size: 14px;
  font-weight: 700;
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: box-shadow 0.15s ease, border-color 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease;
}

/* Hover lifts the glass rather than flattening it to solid white: the chip's two insets
   are restated so the sheen survives, with the ambient shadow deepened. Restating is
   required — `box-shadow` is one property, so naming it here at all replaces the mixin's
   whole stack. */
.md-speak-menu__phrase-page-btn:hover:not([disabled]),
.la-board-actions__scroll-btn:hover:not([disabled]) {
  border-color: rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.5);
  color: #1b2a4a;
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.95), inset 0 -2px 4px rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.1), 0 5px 12px rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.26);
}

.md-speak-menu__phrase-page-btn[disabled],
.la-board-actions__scroll-btn[disabled] {
  opacity: 0.4;
  cursor: default;
}

.md-speak-menu__phrase-pager-status {
  font-size: 13px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: #5A6A85;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

/* Chevron points DOWN collapsed (there is more below) and flips UP when expanded. */
/* Two lines max. With the list paged rather than scrolled, the panel's height is
   row-count x row-height — so a row that can grow without limit puts the height back out of
   control and, on a short viewport, pushes the panel into a scroll region an eye-gaze user
   cannot reach. Two lines is ~90 characters at this size, which covers most saved phrases;
   the full text is still what gets spoken when the row is selected. */
.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn--remembered .md-speak-menu__remembered-text {
  font-size: 16px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: #1B365D;
  display: -webkit-box;
  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
  -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
  overflow: hidden;
}

.md-speak-menu__bottom-btn svg {
  flex-shrink: 0;
  color: inherit;
  opacity: 0.7;
}

/* Legacy list fallback */
.md-speak-menu__legacy-list {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 8px 0;
}

.md-speak-menu__legacy-list li {
  padding: 0;
}

.md-speak-menu__legacy-list a {
  display: block;
  padding: 12px 16px;
  color: #1B365D;
  font-size: 16px;
  font-weight: 500;
  text-decoration: none;
  border-radius: 10px;
  transition: background 0.12s ease;
}

.md-speak-menu__legacy-list a:hover {
  background: rgba(42, 157, 143, 0.06);
}

.md-speak-menu__legacy-context {
  padding: 12px 16px 4px;
  font-size: 14px;
  color: rgba(75, 78, 92, 0.55);
}

/* NO ROW RE-WRAPPING AT 768px. There used to be a `@media (max-width: 768px)` block here
   dropping --3 to 2 columns, --5 to 3 and --7 to 4, on the reasoning that narrow screens
   need bigger touch targets.

   It did the opposite of helping. The modal is a FIXED 600px wide, so re-wrapping did not
   make the modal fit the screen — it made the BUTTONS BIGGER inside the same box and the
   box taller. Measured at 768px: punctuation went 7 columns x 72px to 4 columns x 134px,
   Actions 5 x 106px to 3 x 181px, and the menu grew from 705px to 834px and ran off the
   bottom of a 900px viewport. Wider buttons the user cannot reach because the panel they
   sit in has left the screen are not an accessibility win.

   The width rule below handles narrow screens the right way round: the MODAL takes a
   larger share of the viewport as the viewport shrinks, and the columns stay put. */
/* Speak menu dialog width. Same shape as every other modal in this file
   (`.modal-dialog:has(.la-…-wrap) { width: …; max-width: 94vw; }`) — a design width that
   yields to the viewport rather than a fixed one that overflows it. At 900px the menu uses
   67% of the screen, at 768px 78%, and below ~640px it is 94% throughout. */
.modal-dialog:has(#speak_menu) {
  width: 600px;
  max-width: 94vw;
  /* Bootstrap's `margin: 30px auto` is dropped on the vertical axis. speak-menu.js sets
     `top` on this element and the stylesheet sizes .md-speak-menu with
     `100vh - var(--sm-menu-top)` — a margin the JS does not know about is a second,
     invisible offset stacked on top of the one being compensated for, and the panel then
     hangs off the bottom by exactly that much. Measured at a 640px viewport: dialog
     starting at y=30 with a 624px max-height, so the last 14px sat below the fold with the
     Save Phrase row in it. The horizontal `auto` is kept below — that is what centres it. */
  margin-top: 0;
  margin-bottom: 0;
  /* Centred at EVERY width. Bootstrap only supplies `margin: 30px auto` from 768px up;
     below that its rule is a flat `margin: 10px`, which was harmless while the dialog was
     `width: auto` and filled the space, and is not once a width is declared here — the
     panel would sit hard against the left with all the slack on the right. Stated on both
     sides rather than relying on the shorthand a breakpoint away. */
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
}

/* Small screens: fill the width, 4px each side — same treatment as the Share Text modal.
   The 600px design width plus the 94vw cap left 20px a side at 640 and 11px at 375, and
   84px a side at 768 where the fixed 600px still wins. On a phone that is space the
   punctuation row and the phrase list can use.
   No `!important` needed here, unlike the Share Text modal: this modal passes no
   `@desired_width`, so modal-dialog.js#stretch takes its else branch and CLEARS the inline
   width rather than setting one. `width: auto` is .modal-dialog's Bootstrap default and
   fills the container minus margins — `100vw` would include the scrollbar and overflow.
   Breakpoint is 900px, not Bootstrap's 767px: at 768 the fixed 600px still won and left
   84px a side, which is the tablet-portrait case and the one that was reported. Above
   900px the menu keeps its 600px design width — a 7-column punctuation row stretched
   across a desktop screen is not an improvement. One number if that balance is wrong. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .modal-dialog:has(#speak_menu) {
    width: auto;
    max-width: none;
    margin-left: 4px;
    margin-right: 4px;
  }
}
/* WRAP AT 460px, not 768. Below this the modal has run out of room to widen — it is
   already at its 94vw ceiling, so ~432px of dialog — and 7 punctuation targets across that
   is ~55px each, genuinely too small to hit with the motor accuracy this product is for.
   Here, and only here, re-wrapping is the right trade: the buttons that remain get wider
   because there is nowhere left for the PANEL to grow.

   460px is this codebase's existing narrow breakpoint (`viewport_very_narrow` in
   controllers/user/board-detail.js, and other @media blocks in this file already key off
   it) rather than a number invented for this rule.

