/* Badge-Awarded modal header — 26px top / 30px bottom (requested 2026-09-14), sides left at
   the shared 24px. Arrived at by steps: 64 (a doubling of the shared 32), then 38, then 34
   top, then 8px off each.
   THE 4px GAP BETWEEN TOP AND BOTTOM IS DELIBERATE, not a typo: the <h3> carries a 60px badge
   avatar pulled out of flow by `margin: -15px 10px -25px -5px` (badge-awarded.hbs:8), which
   hangs 10px further below the text box than above it, so equal padding does not read as
   equal. The pair has kept that 4px offset through every revision — preserve it if these
   move again.
   Scoped via `:has(.md-badge-awarded)` like the button rule below, so only this modal
   changes and every other modal keeps `.md-modal-header`'s 32px (app.scss:89338).
   This header is the one that needs it: its <h3> carries a 60px badge avatar pulled out of
   flow with `margin: -15px 10px -25px -5px` (badge-awarded.hbs:8), so the image overhangs
   the header box top and bottom and crowds the shared padding in a way no other modal does.
   Longhands, not the `padding` shorthand, so the shared 24px sides keep coming from the
   base rule rather than being restated here and left to drift.
   Unscoped by scheme on purpose — this applies in Gentle and Focused, classic and modern. */
.modal-content:has(.md-badge-awarded) .md-modal-header {
  padding-top: 26px;
  padding-bottom: 30px;
}

/* Badge-Awarded modal progress bar — the modern bar treatment (requested 2026-09-14).
   This was raw Bootstrap: a 4px-radius #f5f5f5 trough with an inset shadow and a #337ab7
   fill. The values below are the same ones the caseload badge bar uses
   (_caseload.scss:4024-4040) — 999px track, navy-at-0.10 fill with a 0.14 rim, and the
   verdigris ramp — so the two bars in the app read as one component.
   HEIGHT IS THE ONE DEPARTURE: the caseload bar is 10px because it sits in a dense list
   row; this one is the focal element of a modal at 200px wide, where 10px reads as a
   hairline, so it takes 14px.
   `box-shadow: none` on both is load-bearing — Bootstrap puts an inset shadow on each, and
   they read as grime under a flat modern fill.
   Scoped like the button rule below, so no other modal's progress bar moves. Unscoped by
   scheme on purpose: this applies in Gentle and Focused, classic and modern. */
.modal-content:has(.md-badge-awarded) .progress {
  height: 14px;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.1);
  border: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.14);
  overflow: hidden;
  box-shadow: none;
}

.modal-content:has(.md-badge-awarded) .progress .progress-bar {
  height: 100%;
  border-radius: 999px;
  /* THE ACTIVE-TAB RAMP (requested 2026-09-14) — the same two stops as
     `.ch-tab.is-active` in `_focused-view.scss`, so the badge bars and the classic home
     page's selected tab are one gradient.
     90deg, not the tab's 135deg: the stops are the request, the angle is a function of the
     shape. A diagonal across a 14px-tall bar puts the whole transition in a short corner
     sweep; horizontal runs it along the axis the value is actually read on.
     It also measures BETTER than the verdigris ramp it replaces. Against the modal's
     #E8EBEF track the old fill's light stop was 2.78:1 — under the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 asks of
     a non-text UI element — and both of these stops clear it, at 3.92 and 4.13. */
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, #367D8E 0%, #536FAE 100%);
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* Badge-Awarded modal "New Goal" button — uses .md-modal-btn--primary.
   Scoped via `:has(.md-badge-awarded)` so only this modal is affected.
   Same $brand-slate-blue fill as the rest of the primary CTA family. */
.modal-content:has(.md-badge-awarded) .md-modal-btn--primary,
.modal-content:has(.md-badge-awarded) .md-modal-btn--primary:hover,
.modal-content:has(.md-badge-awarded) .md-modal-btn--primary:focus,
.modal-content:has(.md-badge-awarded) .md-modal-btn--primary:focus-visible,
.modal-content:has(.md-badge-awarded) .md-modal-btn--primary:active {
  background-color: #5A6A85 !important;
  background-image: none !important;
  color: #fff !important;
}

/* Badge-Awarded modal — modern empty state shown when the modal opens
   without any badge data (e.g. via the dev console for testing).
   Friendly trophy + encouragement copy instead of a blank body. */
/* THE GENTLE-ONLY SOFTENED FILL IS GONE (2026-09-14). It overrode both bars with
   `#68B5A9 -> #1F7A72` purely to soften the verdigris ramp, and carried a long note
   documenting that it took the fill from 2.62:1 to 1.89:1 against the track — an explicit
   trade, below the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 asks of a non-text UI element.
   Both halves of that are obsolete: the ramp it softened has been replaced, and keeping the
   override would have meant Gentle never showed the new gradient at all. The trade goes with
   it — the fill now measures 3.90-4.32 on the card and 3.92-4.13 in the modal, so the bar is
   above the guideline in both schemes for the first time. */
/* BadgeProgress, `@modern` variant — classic-view's communicator cards only.
   The bar is the SAME treatment as the Badge In Progress modal (999px track, navy-at-0.10
   fill with a 0.14 rim, verdigris ramp), so the card and the modal it opens are one
   component rather than two lookalikes. The legacy inline styling it replaces was a 30px-tall
   Bootstrap bar with a 2px near-black border and a mustard `progress-bar-warning` fill.
   FLEX, replacing the float. The image used to `float: left` with negative margins to sit
   beside the bar; that only worked while the bar was 30px tall, and at 14px it would have
   left the bar pinned to the top of the 40px image. A flex row with `align-items: center`
   centres them against each other at any height.
   `.progress-bar` is reached through two ancestors so it outranks Bootstrap's
   `.progress-bar-warning` background-color (0,1,0) without !important. `box-shadow: none` on
   both kills Bootstrap's inset shadows, which read as grime under a flat fill.
   No scheme scoping — this applies in Gentle and Focused alike. */
.badge-progress--modern > a {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 10px;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.badge-progress--modern img {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 40px;
  height: 40px;
  object-fit: contain;
  object-position: center;
}

.badge-progress--modern .progress {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-width: 0;
  height: 14px;
  margin: 0;
  border-radius: 999px;
  /* A LIGHT TRACK ON THE CARD, where the modal keeps its navy-at-0.10 one. The two sit on
     very different grounds: the modal's bar is on white, so a faint navy track reads as the
     empty half; this bar is on a communicator card, which is a tinted chip in Gentle and a
     mid-tone panel in Focused. Against those, a 0.10 navy track is nearly the card's own
     luminance, and the new fill — which is darker than the old verdigris — measured 2.82:1
     on the Gentle chip and 1.30:1 on the Focused panel, both under the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 asks
     of a non-text UI element.
     A translucent WHITE track inverts that: the empty half reads as light on both cards and
     the dark fill separates from it at 4.32 and 3.66. The 1px rim below is what keeps the
     track itself perceivable against the card. */
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72);
  border: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.14);
  overflow: hidden;
  box-shadow: none;
}

.badge-progress--modern .progress .progress-bar {
  height: 100%;
  border-radius: 999px;
  /* Same ramp as the modal above — the card and the modal it opens stay one component. */
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, #367D8E 0%, #536FAE 100%);
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* The badge artwork block on the in-progress branch — wrapper AND image both 175px tall
   (requested 2026-09-14), down from the 200px the image used to carry inline.
   175 on BOTH, so the wrapper is exactly the image and contributes no dead space of its own.
   That replaces the earlier `margin-bottom: -16px`, which was a way to trim the block before
   an exact height was specified; keeping both would have stacked a negative margin on top of
   a fixed height and taken the block to 159.
   `height` + `width: auto`, never a fixed width: the placeholder SVG is square, but a real
   badge's artwork is any aspect at all, and pinning both axes would squash it. `object-fit`
   is belt-and-braces for the same reason.
   `display: block` also removes the inline-baseline descender gap — an <img> is inline by
   default, so its line box reserves room for descenders a replaced element never draws,
   which is why this block always measured a few px taller than the image. `margin: 0 auto`
   re-centres it, since the parent's `text-align: center` only reaches inline content.
   The inline `width: 200px` had to come OFF both images in this branch: an inline style
   outranks this rule, and 200 wide against 175 tall would have distorted the square SVG.
   Scoped to this class — the EARNED branch keeps its 200px and its medal/ribbon layout. */
.md-badge-awarded__art {
  height: 175px;
}

.md-badge-awarded__art img {
  display: block;
  margin: 0 auto;
  height: 175px;
  width: auto;
  max-width: 100%;
  object-fit: contain;
}

/* "To earn this badge, <user> needs to: ..." — the criteria block under the progress bar.
   75% OF THE MODAL (requested 2026-09-14), replacing an inline `max-width: 300px` that held
   it to a 300px column with ~125px of dead space either side however wide the modal grew.
   `width`, not `max-width`: this has to FILL 75%, and a max- only caps it.
   The percentage resolves against the centred wrapper this sits in, which is a full-width
   child of `.md-modal-body` — so 75% here is 75% of the modal's content box.
   Needs no `:has(.md-badge-awarded)` scoping: the class is already component-specific.
   All views, all modes — no scheme scoping, same as the rest of this block. */
.md-badge-awarded__explain {
