/* ── PERSISTENT APP SHELL (2026-09-21) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
   The wrapper that `templates/application.hbs` puts around the account rail, the primary
   pill nav and the outlet, now that all three are mounted ONCE above the routes instead
   of inside each page.

   THE RAIL POSITIONS ITSELF. `.md-acct-rail` has been `position: fixed; left: 0; bottom: 0`
   with a 208px width since 2026-09-18, so nothing here places it — this only has to keep
   the main column clear of it and stop the nav scrolling away.

   `.ll-appshell__navbar` is `sticky`, not `fixed`, and that is deliberate: `#content` is
   the app's scroll container (it is `position: fixed; overflow` on the page-footer
   layout), so a `fixed` nav would be positioned against the VIEWPORT and would not move
   with the shell's own left offset, while `sticky` resolves against the scroller it
   lives in. It needs an opaque background or the content scrolls visibly underneath it. */
.ll-appshell__main {
  margin-left: var(--ll-rail-width);
  min-width: 0; /* a flex/grid child with long content would otherwise force the page wider */
}

.ll-appshell__main {
  /* NO top padding. It used to hold a 20px gap above the nav, but the nav is now pulled
     out of flow (below) so the PAGE starts at the very top of this column -- see the note
     on `.md-shell` further down for why. */
  padding-top: 0;
}

.ll-appshell__navbar {
  /* BELOW 901px THIS STAYS IN FLOW. At 900px and under the rail stops being a fixed panel and
     becomes an in-flow band above the page, and the pill row gives way to its collapsed
     <details> drawer -- a nav floating over that band, or a drawer expanding out of flow,
     would each be worse than the variance being fixed above. The desktop anchoring is in the
     `min-width: 901px` block further down; everything in THIS rule is the behaviour that
     shipped earlier on 2026-09-21, unchanged. */
  position: sticky;
  /* NOT `top: 0`. Sticky resolves against `#content`, whose padding box starts at y=0
     BEHIND the fixed 70px app navbar, so `0` pinned the pills underneath it. This is the
     same `max(var(--topbar-height, 70px), 70px)` the rail uses to sit below that bar, and
     for the same reason its comment gives: on an authenticated page `--topbar-height` is
     16px (it describes #content's padding, not the bar), and the fallback inside the
     var() matters because an unset variable would otherwise invalidate the whole max(). */
  /* FLUSH WITH THE NAVBAR (requested 2026-10-10: "the top blur line should be flush with the
     bottom of the navbar"). Chrome measures this offset from INSIDE `#content`'s top padding,
     not from its top edge (measured: padding 26px + 70 = the bar drawn at 96 under a header that
     ends at 70; 16px pages drew it at 86). That padding is `--topbar-height` plus a per-page
     extra (0 or 1rem), so both are taken off and the bar meets the header on every page. The
     extra comes from `--ll-content-pad-extra`, set beside the padding in each rule that wins it;
     a page that sets neither keeps its extra as a gap rather than sliding under the header.
     Named so the in-flow ordering at 900px and below can give the bar the same margin. */
  --ll-navbar-sticky-top: calc(max(var(--topbar-height, 70px), 70px) - var(--topbar-height, 0px) - var(--ll-content-pad-extra, 0px));
  top: var(--ll-navbar-sticky-top);
  /* Above page content, below the app navbar and any modal. */
  z-index: 60;
  /* Opaque-ish, or the content scrolls visibly through the pills. */
  background: inherit;
  backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
  padding-bottom: 6px;
  /* OUT OF FLOW, by exactly its own height. Without this the nav occupied a 48px band
     ABOVE the page, and `.md-shell` -- which paints the app's page wash -- began below it,
     leaving an unpainted strip between the app navbar and the content (reported as "the bg
     from the page is not extending all the way up"). Pulling the nav out lets the shell
     start at the top of the column so its wash covers the full height, with the nav
     floating over it. The clearance the nav needs is given back as shell padding below. */
  margin-bottom: -48px;
}

/* The page then has to start BELOW the floating nav. Base `.md-shell` is `padding-top: 3rem`,
   which is 30px, NOT the 48px this comment claimed until 2026-09-21: `html` is `font-size:
   10px` in this app (measured in the browser), so every rem here is 10px. That was enough when
   the nav sat above the page and is not now: the nav is sticky at
   70px and 48px tall, so its underside is 118 while the shell begins at 16. 112px puts the
   first content at 128 -- clear of the nav with a 10px gap -- and every destination in the
   shell roots on `.md-shell` (home, caseload, organizations, boards, extras, updates), so
   none is missed. `!important` only because the base declaration carries one.

   IT IS NOT WINNING A SPECIFICITY FIGHT. The base rule is `#content .ember-view > .md-shell,
   #content > .md-shell` (~46345) and neither selector can MATCH inside the shell: the chain is
   `#content > .ll-appshell > .ll-appshell__main > .ll-appshell__content > .md-user-layout >
   .md-shell`, with no `.ember-view` parent and no direct-child relationship. So this rule has
   no competitor here, and would lose to that ID selector if anyone ever made it match again.

   THE CLEARANCE IS THE NAV'S, so it goes when the nav does (2026-09-21): the nav renders only
   on the Home section's pages now, and on an account page this 112px was 82px of space held
   for something that is not there. A CUSTOM PROPERTY rather than a second padding rule, so there
   is still exactly ONE declaration of this padding to find and change.
   `.ll-appshell.ll-appshell--no-nav` deliberately carries both classes: that beats the base
   `.ll-appshell` on specificity, so the two values do not depend on which is written first. */
.ll-appshell {
  --ll-nav-clearance: var(--dn-nav-clearance, 112px);
  /* THE RAIL'S WIDTH, stated once (2026-09-28). Read by `.md-acct-rail` (its width),
     `.ll-appshell__main` (margin-left) and the fixed `.ll-appshell__navbar` (left), which were
     three separate 208px literals until the rail became collapsible and had to be able to
     change all three at once. The collapsed value is set in THE COLLAPSED RAIL block. */
  --ll-rail-width: var(--dn-rail-width, 208px);
}

.ll-appshell.ll-appshell--no-nav {
  /* The value the base `.md-shell` rule gives a shell with no nav floating over it -- 3rem,
     i.e. 30px at this app's 10px root -- which is the geometry these pages had before the
     chrome was hoisted above them. Measured before and after on the account pages
     (scripts/home-section-nav-qa.mjs): the shell's first content moved from y=198 to y=116. */
  --ll-nav-clearance: 3rem;
}

.ll-appshell .md-shell {
  padding-top: var(--ll-nav-clearance) !important;
}

/* CASELOAD TAKES NO NAV CLEARANCE (requested 2026-09-21, for Gentle: "on gentle view, the
   caseload page, this top padding needs to be removed" -- the inspector showed
   `.md-shell--caseload` at `112px 0 0`).
   FOCUSED ALREADY DID THIS, at _focused-view.scss:1072, which is why the two views disagreed:
   that page's own top spacing comes from `.md-shell--caseload > .md-workspace`, so the
   shell's clearance was a second helping on top of it. Written here, unscoped, both views now
   agree and the focused rule becomes a no-op rather than the only thing holding the layout.
   THREE CLASSES on purpose: the rule it answers is `.ll-appshell .md-shell` (0,2,0) and
   carries `!important`, so `.md-shell--caseload` alone (0,1,0) could not reach it. */
.ll-appshell .md-shell.md-shell--caseload {
  padding-top: 0 !important;
}

/* ── GENTLE: EVERY HOME-SECTION PAGE STARTS WHERE CASELOAD DOES (2026-09-21) ────────────
   Requested: "on gentle view modern view the dashboard, boards, extras, and updates content
   all needs to begin in the same place it does on the caseload page: make it consistent".
   Measured at 1280 before this, as the y of the first thing actually visible inside the
   workspace: caseload 106, dashboard / boards / extras 218, updates 238. Two separate causes,
   so it takes two rules:
     - the shell's 112px nav clearance, which caseload alone does not take (above); and
     - `#content`'s own top padding, which is `calc(--topbar-height + 1rem)` = 26px on the
       pages that carry a page class and the bare `+ 3rem` = 46px default on Updates, which
       has none. That 20px is the whole of Updates' extra offset.

   `:has(.ll-appshell)` IS THE SCOPE AS OF 2026-09-23 -- it was `:has(.ll-appshell__navbar)`,
   which reached only the pages that render the pill nav. Requested: "make all of the page
   content for account, goals, logs, profile, recordings, reports, settings, subscriptions, and
   supervision move up on the page to the same place it begins on the home page", plus Rooms.
   That is every page in the app shell, so the scope is now the shell itself rather than the nav
   inside it -- one rule for "a page in this shell starts here" instead of two sets that have to
   be kept level with each other.

   MEASURED, at 1280, with scripts/account-pages-top-alignment-qa.mjs. The account pages sat at
   y=156 and Reports at y=136 against the home section's y=106, and the whole of both gaps was
   these two declarations: `#content` at the bare `calc(--topbar-height + 3rem)` = 46px because
   those routes carry no page class (controllers/application.js `content_class` labels only
   stats/home/boards/extras/caseload), and the shell's `--ll-nav-clearance`, which is 3rem =
   30px on `.ll-appshell--no-nav`. 46+30+40+40 = 156, 26+30+40+40 = 136, 26+0+40+40 = 106 --
   the chain reproduces all three, so setting the pair lands every one of them on 106.
   (Reports already had 26px via `#content.stats-page`; only its 30px of clearance moves.)

   GENTLE ONLY, as asked. Focused sets these per page in _focused-view.scss and is untouched. */
body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #within_ember #content:has(.ll-appshell) {
  padding-top: calc(var(--topbar-height) + 1rem) !important;
  /* What this padding adds beyond `--topbar-height`, for the pill bar's sticky offset at 900px and
     below (see `--ll-navbar-sticky-top` on `.ll-appshell__navbar`). `!important` like the padding,
     so whichever rule wins the padding also wins this. */
  --ll-content-pad-extra: 1rem !important;
}

/* Not the Updates arrival (2026-10-10): it takes Focused's positioning in both layouts — the
   shell keeps its nav clearance (`.ll-appshell .md-shell`); see `md-main--updates-arrival` in
   _focused-view.scss. */
body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content:has(.ll-appshell):not(:has(.md-main--updates-arrival)) .ll-appshell .md-shell {
  padding-top: 0 !important;
}

/* ...AND 52px OF IT BACK AT 1024 AND BELOW (requested 2026-09-21: "the main page content
   needs to be pushed down 52px on each of the pages (dashboard, caseload, boards, extras, and
   updates)"). A RELATIVE push, as asked, not a target: the five pages already agree at these
   widths (all five measured at y=66 at 1024, where the workspace's own 40px padding drops to
   0), so adding the same 52px to the shell keeps them agreeing and moves them together.
   On the same `:has(.ll-appshell)` scope as the rule above (widened 2026-09-23), so the account
   pages take the same push and the whole shell stays level at these widths too -- leaving them
   on the old 30px clearance here would have aligned the pages at 1280 and split them again at
   1024, which is the failure this block exists to prevent. */
@media (max-width: 1024px) {
  /* (The 52px moved to the <=900px block at the end of this one, 2026-10-01; see there.) */
  /* NO SIDE GUTTER ON THE WORKSPACE AT THESE WIDTHS (requested 2026-09-21: "the md-workspace
     l/r padding needs to be 0"). It was not one value to halve or zero -- measured at 1024 the
     five pages ran 40px (caseload, dashboard, extras), 14px (boards) and 16px (updates), and
     the steps below diverged further, with extras still at 40px at 820 while everything else
     had stepped down twice. One declaration replaces all of it, so the pages agree.
     `!important` because the base `.md-workspace` padding carries one, and the id in the scope
     supplies the weight to reach past it.
     DELIBERATELY STILL `:has(.ll-appshell__navbar)` after the 2026-09-23 widening around it:
     this is the only HORIZONTAL rule in the block, and the request that widened the others was
     about where content BEGINS down the page. The account pages keep the side padding they have
     always had here. */
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content:has(.ll-appshell__navbar) .md-workspace {
    padding-left: 0 !important;
    padding-right: 0 !important;
  }
  /* BOARDS SITS 24px LOWER THAN THE REST (requested 2026-09-21: "boards content needs to move
     down by 24px"). 52px + 24px, written as the sum's result on the same property the rule
     above sets, so there is one padding value per page rather than a base and a nudge.
     `#content.boards-page` matches the general scope's specificity exactly -- both are
     (1,2,1) once `body:not(...)` is counted -- so this has to come AFTER it to win, which is
     why it is here rather than beside the other boards rules. */
}
/* ...AND BOTH PUSHES START AT 900, NOT 1024 (2026-10-01, requested: "hold the full-size spacing").
   They made up for the workspace's 40px top margin, which used to go at 1024; that margin now holds
   down to 901 (the <=900 block after the general <=1024 block), so above 900 these would push the
   page 12px (36px on Boards) below where it sits at full size. Below 900 nothing changes. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content:has(.ll-appshell):not(:has(.md-main--updates-arrival)) .ll-appshell .md-shell {
    padding-top: 52px !important;
  }
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content.boards-page .ll-appshell .md-shell {
    padding-top: 76px !important;
  }
  /* THE HOME PAGE HAS NONE (requested 2026-10-10: "on 900px and lower, modern view, the top
     padding of the md-shell on the home page needs to be removed"). This clearance kept content
     out from under a pill bar that floated over the shell; at these widths the bar and the
     account-rail dropdown now sit above the shell in the flow, so on the dashboard it was only a
     gap. Same two selectors as the Focused dashboard rule below (the dashboard answers to
     `home-page` and to `index`, and Extras also carries `index`). `:has(.ll-appshell)` matches the
     general rule's weight, (1,5,1): without it this lost to that rule, as the Boards line above
     still does (Boards measures 52px, not 76). */
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content.home-page:has(.ll-appshell) .ll-appshell .md-shell,
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content.index:not(.extras-page):has(.ll-appshell) .ll-appshell .md-shell {
    padding-top: 0 !important;
  }
}
/* NO TOP PADDING OR MARGIN ON THE WORKSPACE BELOW 901px (requested 2026-09-21, Gentle:
   "remove the top padding and top margins from md-workspace (on all pages, dashboard,
   caseload, organizations, boards, extras, and updates"). Like the side gutter above, this
   was never one value: measured across those six pages the top padding ran 0 to 52px and the
   margin 0 to 32px, each page carrying its own history. Both go to 0 so they agree.
   The shell's own top padding (52px, 76px on boards) is untouched -- that is what still holds
   the content clear of the chrome; this only removes the SECOND gap stacked inside it.
   Widened to `:has(.ll-appshell)` on 2026-09-23 with the rest of this block, so the account
   pages lose the same second gap rather than keeping one the six named pages do not. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content:has(.ll-appshell) .md-workspace {
    padding-top: 0 !important;
    margin-top: 0 !important;
    /* AND NONE ON `.md-main` INSIDE IT (requested 2026-10-10: "modern gentle view on 900px and
       smaller, remove the top padding from md-main"). Its base rule reads this variable (18px by
       default; Compressed View sets 36px on the home shell), so it is set here, on the workspace
       that holds `.md-main`, which is closer than the shell and so wins in both densities. */
    --dn-main-pad-top: 0px;
  }
}
/* ── THE ORGANIZATIONS DIRECTORY CLEARS THE NAV, IN BOTH LAYOUTS (2026-09-24) ───────────
   Requested as two moves in opposite directions: "gentle organizations - move the content down
   on the page" and "focused organizations - move the content up on the page (significantly)".
   They meet in the middle, and the measurement says why.

   MEASURED AT 1280, Modern `/organizations`, as the y of the page hero:
     gentle  #content 26 + shell   0 + workspace 20/40 -> hero at  86
     focused #content 46 + shell 112 + workspace 20/40 -> hero at 218
   The floating pill nav occupies 70-146. So in Gentle the hero was UNDER the nav -- this page's
   first element is its title, with none of the leading whitespace the dashboard's cards have, so
   it was the one page where the shell's deliberate nav overlap actually collided with content.
   In Focused the full 112px clearance plus this page's own insets pushed it 72px past where it
   needed to be.

   ONE TARGET FOR BOTH: 156, which is 10px under the nav's underside. That is why the two
   requests resolve to one rule with two values rather than to two independent nudges -- the
   page wants to sit just below the nav, and each layout was missing it from a different side.
   The values are the arithmetic, not a taste: gentle needs 156 - 86 = 70, focused needs
   156 - 106 = 50 (focused's `#content` already carries 20px more than gentle's).

   ON THE SHELL, not the workspace: the workspace's 20/40 is this page's own inner rhythm and is
   shared with the org DETAIL pages, which were not asked about and do not have this problem.
   `!important` matched, not added -- the rules being overridden
   (`.ll-appshell .md-shell`, and the gentle `:has(.ll-appshell)` block above) both carry one. */
body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content .ll-appshell .md-shell.md-shell--org-directory {
  padding-top: 70px !important;
}

body.ll-layout-focused #content .ll-appshell .md-shell.md-shell--org-directory {
  padding-top: 50px !important;
}

/* FOCUSED DASHBOARD: 32px OFF THE TOP BELOW 901px (requested 2026-09-21: "reduce the top
   padding on the dashboard content by 32px"). 112px -> 80px.
   THE SHELL'S CLEARANCE IS THE ONLY TOP PADDING LEFT AT THESE WIDTHS, which is why it is the
   one reduced: measured on the focused dashboard at 900 and 820, `.md-workspace` is already
   `padding-top: 0; margin-top: 0` and `.md-main` is 0, so the 112px nav clearance is the whole
   gap. Taking the 32px off anything else would have had no effect.
   TWO SELECTORS, because the dashboard answers to two routes and `content_class`
   (controllers/application.js) labels them differently: `user.home` gets `home-page`, while
   the top-level `index` route gets `index`. The `:not(.extras-page)` is load-bearing -- the
   extras page ALSO carries `index` (its live class list is "index modern-dashboard
   extras-page"), so a bare `#content.index` would have moved Extras too. */
/* THEN TO 0 (requested 2026-10-10: "the top padding of the md-shell on the home page needs to be
   removed"). The pill bar and the account-rail dropdown now sit above the shell in the flow at
   these widths, so there is nothing left for this clearance to clear. Gentle's home is set to
   match beside its 52px general rule. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  body.ll-layout-focused #content.home-page .ll-appshell .md-shell,
  body.ll-layout-focused #content.index:not(.extras-page) .ll-appshell .md-shell {
    padding-top: 0 !important;
  }
}
/* BOARDS, EXTRAS AND UPDATES START WHERE THE DASHBOARD DOES, BELOW 901px (requested
   2026-09-21, Focused: "the boards, extras, and updates content all need to move up to the
   same level as the dashboard content").
   THREE INDEPENDENT OFFSETS STACK HERE, which is why one declaration could not do it.
   Measured at 900, the content y was dashboard 361, extras 425, updates 443, boards 473, and
   the gap is made of three different things:
     - `#content`'s own top padding: 16px on the dashboard and extras, 46px on updates, 52px
       on boards -- which moves the whole appshell, rail band included;
     - the shell's nav clearance: 80px on the dashboard (reduced earlier today), 112px on the
       other three;
     - `.md-workspace`'s top padding: 0 on the dashboard, 20px updates, 32px extras, 44px
       boards.
   All three are set to the dashboard's values, so the four pages compute the same sum rather
   than being nudged to look equal at one width: 16 + rail + nav + 80 + 0.

   CASELOAD IS DELIBERATELY NOT INCLUDED. It was not named, and it sits 16px ABOVE the
   dashboard (345 at 900), so folding it in would have MOVED it to satisfy a request about
   three other pages.
   UPDATES HAS NO PAGE CLASS to select, unlike boards and extras, so it is identified by what
   is true of it alone: it is the only `.md-shell--user` page that renders the pill nav -- the
   account pages share the shell class but have no nav.
   `#within_ember` for the two-id weight: the `#content` padding these override is itself set
   by `#within_ember #content...` rules at (2,1,0), which a one-id selector cannot reach. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  body.ll-layout-focused #within_ember #content.boards-page,
  body.ll-layout-focused #within_ember #content.extras-page,
  body.ll-view-modern #within_ember #content:has(.ll-appshell__navbar):has(.md-shell--user) {
    padding-top: var(--topbar-height) !important;
    --ll-content-pad-extra: 0px !important; /* see the Gentle `#content:has(.ll-appshell)` rule */
  }
  body.ll-layout-focused #within_ember #content.boards-page .ll-appshell .md-shell,
  body.ll-layout-focused #within_ember #content.extras-page .ll-appshell .md-shell,
  body.ll-view-modern #within_ember #content:has(.ll-appshell__navbar):has(.md-shell--user) .ll-appshell .md-shell {
    padding-top: 80px !important;
  }
  body.ll-layout-focused #within_ember #content.boards-page .md-workspace,
  body.ll-layout-focused #within_ember #content.extras-page .md-workspace,
  body.ll-view-modern #within_ember #content:has(.ll-appshell__navbar):has(.md-shell--user) .md-workspace {
    padding-top: 0 !important;
  }
}
/* THE NAV FITS ITS CONTENT. `.md-pillnav` is a block-level flex container with no width of
   its own, so it stretched to the shell's full 1232px (capped by its own 1120px max-width)
   once mounted here instead of inside each page's narrower column. `fit-content` restores
   the pill-bar look; the base rule's `margin: 42px auto 0` then centres it, and that 42px
   top is zeroed because this wrapper owns the vertical spacing now. */
.ll-appshell__navbar .md-pillnav {
  width: fit-content !important;
  margin-top: 0 !important;
}

/* The legacy Bootstrap `.row` wrapper inside the dashboard carries `margin: 0 -20px -10px`,
   the classic negative gutter meant to cancel a parent's 20px padding. Inside the app shell
   there is no such padding to cancel, so it simply made the page 40px wider than its column
   and pushed its left edge 15px UNDER the fixed rail -- which read as the rail's background
   not reaching the panel's full width. Neutralised here rather than at the source: that
   negative margin is still doing its original job everywhere else the row is used. */
.ll-appshell__content > .row {
  margin-left: 0 !important;
  margin-right: 0 !important;
  /* The matching 5px side padding goes with them. It is the other half of the same
     Bootstrap gutter convention (negative margin on the row, padding back on the row/col),
     so leaving it behind just inset the page by 5px against nothing. */
  padding-left: 0 !important;
  padding-right: 0 !important;
}

/* At 900px and below the rail stops being a fixed panel and becomes an in-flow block above
   the page (see the rail's own breakpoint rules), so the offset that clears it has to go or
   the content is pushed off-screen on a tablet held in portrait. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .ll-appshell__main {
    margin-left: 0;
  }
  /* THE PILLS SIT 16px LOWER BELOW 900 (requested 2026-09-21, both Gentle and Focused:
     "the pillnav needs to be pushed down 16px (while keeping the bottom content bg above
     it)"). PADDING, not `margin-top` or `top`: padding is inside the element's background
     box, so the frosted band grows upward by the same 16px and covers the space the pills
     vacated -- which is the parenthesis in the request. A margin would have moved the band
     with the pills and left a bare strip above it.
     This width is below the `min-width: 901px` block that anchors the nav to the app frame,
     so here it is still the in-flow sticky element and this simply adds to its box. */
  .ll-appshell__navbar {
    padding-top: 16px;
  }
}
/* ── THE NAV SITS IN ONE PLACE ON EVERY PAGE (2026-09-21) ───────────────────────────────
   Reported: "all pillnav menus need to show in the same place on the page ... they are
   varying all over the place on modern focused view". Measured on Modern + Focused at
   1280x900, the nav's top was 86 on Dashboard, Caseload and Extras, 96 on Boards, and 116 on
   Organizations and Updates -- a 30px spread on a nav that is mounted ONCE precisely so it
   does not move between pages.

   THE CAUSE, traced rather than guessed: `position: sticky` offsets from the SCROLL
   CONTAINER's padding box, and that container is `#content`, whose `padding-top` is set
   per-page -- `calc(var(--topbar-height) + 3rem)` = 46px by default, `+ 1rem` = 26px for
   `.boards-page` / `.modern-dashboard` / `.stats-page`, and `var(--topbar-height)` = 16px for
   the focused index. At rest the nav sits AT that padding edge, so its screen position was
   the page's own top padding + 70. Each measurement above is exactly that sum.

   THE FIX IS THE RAIL'S. `.md-acct-rail` (~49712) has been `position: fixed; top:
   max(var(--topbar-height, 70px), 70px); left: 0` since 2026-09-18, which is why the rail
   held still on all six pages while the nav wandered. Anchoring the nav to the same app frame
   makes the two halves of the chrome one thing. See that rule for why the offset is a `max()`
   with the fallback INSIDE the `var()`.

   NOTHING ELSE MOVES. In flow the nav occupied a 48px band and cancelled it with
   `margin-bottom: -48px`, a net contribution of 0; fixed, it contributes 0 as well. The
   `+ 16px` is the gap the Dashboard already had (86 = 70 + 16), and `left: 208px; right: 0`
   is the same band `.ll-appshell__main` gives it, so `width: fit-content` centres the pills
   exactly where they were. Verified: left 390/392 and width 704/707 before and after.

   ABOVE 900px ONLY, matching the rail's own breakpoint: below it the rail is in flow and the
   pill row has given way to its collapsed drawer, so the nav belongs in the document there. */
@media (min-width: 901px) {
  .ll-appshell__navbar {
    position: fixed;
    /* THE FROSTED BAND REACHES THE APP NAVBAR (requested 2026-09-21: "the frosted band from
       this div needs to extend all the way up and 12px down from where it is"). It used to
       start 16px below the navbar, leaving a strip of page content showing unfrosted between
       the two. The 16px did not disappear -- it moved from `top` into `padding-top`, so the
       BAND now starts at the navbar's underside while the pills inside it stay exactly where
       they were at y=86. Changing `top` alone would have moved the pills with it. */
    top: max(var(--topbar-height, 70px), 70px);
    padding-top: var(--dn-nav-pad-top, 16px);
    /* 0: THE BAND ENDS AT THE BOTTOM OF THE PILLS (requested 2026-10-10: "the blur underneath the
       main pill-nav menu stretches too far downward -> make it only stretch to the bottom of the
       pill-nav menu"). It was 18px (6px base + 12px asked for on 2026-09-21), which carried the
       blur 18px past the pills and over the top of the page content. Only the band's bottom edge
       moves: the pills are placed by `top` + `padding-top` above, so they stay at y=86. */
    padding-bottom: var(--dn-nav-pad-bottom, 0);
    left: var(--ll-rail-width);
    right: 0;
    /* The in-flow band it cancelled no longer exists. */
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
}
/* ── BOOTSTRAP 3'S TOUCH-ONLY DROPDOWN BACKDROP IS DISABLED (2026-09-24) ─────────────────
   REPORTED as "when you are on the account page and click the extras dropdown, it disables
   scrolling", then narrowed by the reporter to "only when i'm in devtools". It is NOT a DevTools
   bug, and that distinction is the point: DevTools' device toolbar makes Chrome advertise touch
   support, and Bootstrap 3 inserts `.dropdown-backdrop` ONLY when `'ontouchstart' in
   document.documentElement`. The device toolbar does not cause the fault -- it reproduces on a
   desktop what every REAL touch device already has, which for this product means the iPads and
   touchscreen Chromebooks a large share of users run.

   WHAT IT DOES. The backdrop is a `position: fixed` overlay across the whole viewport at
   z-index 990. Measured with touch emulation on: `elementFromPoint` at the centre of the page
   returns `div.dropdown-backdrop`, and a wheel gesture leaves `#content.scrollTop` exactly where
   it was (277 -> 277). With it gone the same gesture scrolls normally (277 -> 677).

   WHY IT IS SAFE TO DROP. It is a workaround for iOS 5/6 Safari, where a `click` on a
   non-interactive element did not bubble to `document`, so Bootstrap could not see the tap
   outside that should close an open menu. Bootstrap's own `clearMenus` handler on `document`
   does that job in every browser this app supports. Verified, not assumed, with touch emulation
   and the backdrop suppressed: an outside mouse click closes the menu, and so does a real
   touchscreen tap.

   `display: none` rather than `pointer-events: none`: the element would still be in the way of
   anything that hit-tests by walking the tree, and a backdrop that cannot be clicked is not a
   backdrop.

   AT THE END OF THE FILE ON PURPOSE. This overrides a rule in Bootstrap's own vendor.css, which
   the app loads BEFORE frontend.css, so equal specificity here is decided by sheet order and
   this wins without `!important`. It is last in this file so nothing later can tie it.
   (A first attempt put it beside the other dropdown rules and landed INSIDE the selector on the
   line above, silently breaking `header #normal_mode #level_dropdown ~ .dropdown-menu` -- which
   is why this one is appended rather than threaded into the middle of a 120k-line stylesheet.) */
.dropdown-backdrop {
  display: none;
}

/* ── FOCUSED: "COMPARE REPORT PERIODS" IN DARK BLUE, NOT TEAL (2026-09-24) ───────────────
   Requested: "on focused view, give the COMPARE REPORT PERIODS a dark blue hue instead of teal".
   The badge's gentle styling is a teal wash with `#0d5c5f` ink and a teal glow; every teal-bearing
   property is restated here in navy/denim so nothing is left half-converted -- the fill, the ink,
   and the shadow that was tinting the area around it.
   GENTLE IS UNTOUCHED: the base rule keeps its teal, which is the palette the rest of that view
   uses. */
body.ll-layout-focused .md-stats-compare-filter-group__badge {
  color: #1B365D;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35) 0%, rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.1) 35%, rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.22) 100%);
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4), 0 2px 12px rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.15);
}

/* ── COMMUNICATOR PICKER: SEARCH FILTER (2026-09-24) ─────────────────────────────────────
   The box only renders where a call site asks for it (`@filterable`), which today is the
   Select User for Reports modal -- see components/user-select.hbs.
   THE LABEL IS VISIBLE, not a placeholder: placeholder text vanishes the moment someone starts
   typing, which is when a switch or screen-magnifier user most needs to still see what the
   field is for. It is small and quiet rather than hidden.
   14px on the input is the floor this product holds text to, and it also stops iOS Safari
   zooming the viewport when the field takes focus -- anything under 16px normally would, but
   the modal is not a form people live in and the zoom is the lesser problem than a label
   nobody can read. */
.user-select-filter {
  margin: 0 0 12px;
}

.user-select-filter__label {
  display: block;
  margin: 0 0 4px;
  font-size: 14px;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.72);
}

.user-select-filter__input {
  width: 100%;
  height: 40px;
  padding: 0 12px;
  font-size: 14px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.18);
  border-radius: 10px;
  background: #fff;
  box-shadow: inset 0 1px 2px rgba(27, 54, 93, 0.05);
}

.user-select-filter__input:focus {
  outline: 2px solid rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.55);
  outline-offset: 1px;
  border-color: rgba(76, 134, 216, 0.5);
}

/* Spans the grid so the message sits under the search box rather than in the first card slot. */
.user-select-filter__empty {
  padding: 8px 0 16px;
  text-align: center;
}

/* ── GENTLE ACCOUNT SECTION: THE CONTENT STARTS WITH THE RAIL (2026-09-24) ───────────────
   Requested: "modern gentle My Account page, move the page content up considerably".
   MEASURED at 1280 before this, as the chain down to the first thing on the page:
     #content 26 + shell 0 + workspace 40 margin + 40 padding = main at 106
     + main's own 35 padding = hero at 141, + the hero's 28 = the title near 169.
   Meanwhile the ACCOUNT RAIL beside it starts at y=70, so the page read as though its content
   had been pushed a third of the way down the panel next to it.
   THE WORKSPACE'S 80px IS WHAT GOES -- its 40px margin and 40px padding are the two largest
   terms and the only ones that are pure gap. `.md-main--user`'s 35px and the hero's 28px are the
   CARD's own inset and the header's own rhythm; cutting those would have tightened the content
   against its container rather than moving the block up.
   16px of padding is kept so the card does not start flush against the shell's edge. Result:
   main at 42, hero at 77 -- level with the rail's 70, which is the alignment the page was
   missing.
   GENTLE ONLY, as asked; Focused sets these per page in _focused-view.scss and is untouched.
   BOUNDED AT 901 for the same reason the 1024 step below it is (read that note): under 901 the
   rail drops in flow above the content and the shell-wide `@media (max-width: 900px)` rule at
   the foot of the gentle block takes this box to 0. Leaving the 16px unbounded outweighed that
   rule and held 16px of the 64 this section was asked to give up at those widths. */
@media (min-width: 901px) {
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content:has(.md-hero--dashboard-user) .ll-appshell .md-shell--user > .md-workspace {
    margin-top: 0 !important;
    padding-top: 16px !important;
  }
}
/* ...AND 64px FURTHER UP AT 1024 AND BELOW (requested 2026-09-24: "move it up another 64px" /
   "on 1024px and smaller").
   THE 64 IS NOT ONE NUMBER, it is two, and measuring is what showed that. At 1280 the account
   page's title sits at y=105; at 1024 it sits at 149 -- 44px lower for the same page, because
   the gentle alignment block above gives `.ll-appshell .md-shell` 52px of top padding at this
   breakpoint that desktop does not have. So the move is that 52px, plus 12 of the workspace's
   own 16px inset:
     shell    52 -> 0   (the breakpoint's clearance, which this section does not need: it has no
                         floating pill nav to clear -- that is what the 52px was reserving)
     workspace 16 -> 20
   THEN 16px BACK DOWN, asked for straight after seeing it (net 48 up from 149, title at 101).
   The two steps are folded into the single workspace value rather than left as a 4px rule plus a
   16px correction -- one declaration per box, so the next person reads a position rather than an
   argument. The shell's 52 -> 0 is untouched by that second step.
   The title clears the fixed header either way; its bottom edge is y=70. */
@media (max-width: 1024px) {
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content:not(:has(.md-main--updates-arrival)) .ll-appshell .md-shell.md-shell--user {
    padding-top: 0 !important;
  }
}
/* THE WORKSPACE'S 20px STOPS AT 901. Below that the rail is no longer a side column: it drops
   in flow as a full-width grid ABOVE the content, and the shell-wide rule at the foot of the
   gentle block (`@media (max-width: 900px) ... .md-workspace { padding-top: 0; margin-top: 0 }`)
   already says every page in this shell starts flush there. This rule outweighs it (0,4,1 against
   0,3,1) and was quietly reinstating a gap those six pages had been told to drop, so it is bounded
   rather than repeated -- one statement per range instead of one rule undoing another.
   The shell's `padding-top: 0` above is NOT bounded: it stays 0 all the way down, or the narrow
   widths would take back the 52px push the account section is exempt from. */
@media (min-width: 901px) and (max-width: 1024px) {
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content:has(.md-hero--dashboard-user) .ll-appshell .md-shell--user > .md-workspace {
    padding-top: 20px !important;
  }
}
/* ── 64px UP AT 900 AND BELOW, ACCOUNT SECTION AND REPORTS (2026-09-24) ─────────────────
   Requested as two lines: "modern gentle on 900px and smaller: move account page content up
   64px / move stats page content up 64px".

   WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE BREAKPOINT FROM THE 1024 WORK ABOVE. At 901 and up the account rail is
   a fixed side column and the content begins at the top of the page. Below 901 the rail is in
   flow: a full-width grid of nine links, 202px tall at 900, sitting ABOVE the content. So what
   the eye reads as "the page content" starts under the rail, and the gap complained of is the
   band between the rail's bottom edge and the page title -- measured 95px on the account page
   and 112px on Reports.

   NOTHING IN THAT BAND PAINTS. Checked before cutting, on both pages at 900:
   `.ll-appshell__main`, `.md-shell`, `.md-workspace`, `.md-main--user` and `.md-hero` all report
   a transparent background, no border and no shadow. The band is empty space in four or five
   stacked boxes, so removing it moves the content up without tightening it against any edge --
   the reason the cuts below are safe and the rail's own 20px margin-bottom is left alone.
   THE RAIL IS DELIBERATELY UNTOUCHED, and not only for that: it renders on BOTH pages, so any
   cut to it would count toward both budgets at once and the two moves could not be sized
   independently. Each page's 64 comes out of boxes only that page has.
     account (95 available): workspace 20 -> 0, `.md-main--user` 35 -> 0, title lead 20 -> 11
     Reports (112 available): shell 52 -> 0, hero 24 -> 12
   Each cut lives at the rule that owns the box (see `.md-shell--user > .md-workspace >
   .md-main--user`, `.md-hero.md-hero--dashboard .md-hero__title`, and
   `.md-shell--reports-view .md-hero--reports-dashboard`); only Reports' shell exception is here,
   beside the account one it copies.

   REPORTS' 52px IS THE SAME EXEMPTION THE ACCOUNT PAGES ALREADY HAVE. The gentle block's 1024
   rule pushes every shell page down 52px; the account section was exempted from it above. Reports
   is one of the nine pages of that section (it is the rail's "Reports" row), so zeroing it here
   brings Reports back in line with its own section rather than making it an exception. */
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content .ll-appshell .md-shell.md-shell--stats {
    padding-top: 0 !important;
  }
}
/* ── ABOVE 900: THE SEVEN INNER SECTION PAGES SIT WHERE FOCUSED PUTS THEM (2026-09-25) ──
   Requested: "modern view gentle: on >900px width move the Goals, Logs, Profile, Recordings,
   Preferences, Subscription, & Supervision page contents down ... position them down the same
   level they are on focused view".

   WHICH PAGES, AND HOW THEY ARE TOLD APART. All nine account-section pages share
   `.md-shell--user`, so the class alone cannot separate them. The account page is the one that
   renders `.md-hero--dashboard-user` (templates/user/index.hbs), so `:has()` on it names the
   account page and `:not(:has())` names the other seven. Reports is not in either set: it is a
   bare route with its own `.md-shell--stats` (see the block below).
   The account page keeps the position it was given on 2026-09-24 ("move the page content up
   considerably", then another 64px), which is why the two rules that do that now carry the same
   `:has(.md-hero--dashboard-user)` scope. They were written when the section moved as one; it
   does not any more, and scoping them is the honest way to say so.

   MEASURED IN THE BROWSER, Modern, `/goals` as the representative (all seven agree to the
   pixel), as the y of the page title:
                gentle before   focused   delta
     1280            77           171      +94
     1024            81           131      +50
      950            81           131      +50
   Focused reaches 171/131 as #content 46 + shell 30 + workspace 40/0 margin + 20 padding +
   `.md-main--user` 35. Three of those four are simply the values these pages have when the
   gentle alignment block leaves them alone, so this block restores them rather than inventing
   numbers: #content to the bare `+ 3rem` those routes get without a page class, and the shell
   to its own `--ll-nav-clearance`. THE WORKSPACE MARGIN IS DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED -- the base
   is already 40 at 1280 and 0 at 1024 and below, which is exactly what Focused has at each
   width, and writing it out would freeze one width's value across both.
   Only the padding needs stating, and 20px is the number Focused itself uses.

   GENTLE ONLY and >900 ONLY, as asked. The 900-and-below work of 2026-09-24 (the section moving
   up 64px when the rail drops in flow) is a different range and is untouched. */
@media (min-width: 901px) {
  /* Not Basic (2026-10-01): its rail pages take the home page's `#content` padding so the rail
     starts at one height on every page (_classic-home.scss, "ONE MENU HEIGHT"). */
  /* THE UPDATES ARRIVAL IS EXCLUDED FROM THE SHELL AND WORKSPACE RULES BELOW (2026-10-10). The
     logs page opened from the home pill-nav's Updates (`md-main--updates-arrival`,
     templates/user.hbs) shares `.md-shell--user` with the account pages but, unlike them, HAS the
     floating pill-nav. Cutting the shell's nav clearance lifted its title into the nav's blur band
     (measured 131 against the band's 146 at 1280). It takes Focused's positioning instead (shell
     clearance kept; workspace and main zeroed by the arrival rules in _focused-view.scss). The
     `#content` rule just below is NOT excluded: its 46px is exactly Focused's value. */
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused):not(.ll-view-basic) #within_ember #content:has(.md-shell--user):not(:has(.md-hero--dashboard-user)) {
    padding-top: calc(var(--topbar-height) + 3rem) !important;
  }
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content:has(.md-shell--user):not(:has(.md-hero--dashboard-user)):not(:has(.md-main--updates-arrival)) .ll-appshell .md-shell {
    padding-top: 3rem !important;
  }
  body:not(.ll-layout-focused) #content:has(.md-shell--user):not(:has(.md-hero--dashboard-user)):not(:has(.md-main--updates-arrival)) .ll-appshell .md-shell--user > .md-workspace {
    padding-top: 20px !important;
